#lang ivy1.8

# ============================================================================
# PLAN PHASE 2: the directory, in isolation.
#
# The beat-granular cache is gone from this file (preserved verbatim in
# `cache_beats.ivy`); the memory controller's client is now `dir_body`, the
# German home node, and the only thing on the other side of the coherence
# channels is `mock_body` -- a one-line stand-in for the cache that requests
# lines, stores into what it holds, and writes back what it dirtied.
#
#            mock_body                (phase 3: the real cache)
#          ch1 / ch2 / ch3
#            dir_body                 (grants, dirty writebacks; no
#          four_words beats            invalidates, no sharer array yet)
#            mc_body                  (UNCHANGED)
#          mem_* top-level wires
#
# What is proved here, and why each piece exists:
#
#   [dir_img]    `~cache_owns(H,O) -> dir(H,O) = img(H,O)` -- the phase's
#                headline.  `dir` is the image the directory + controller
#                present; the directory holds no data array (design decision
#                4) and dirty data always round-trips memory (decision 3),
#                so `dir` IS `mmem` and the fact is `mc_owns_img` restated at
#                the seam the client consumes it across.
#   [grant_img]  the payload of a grant is the image at the granted line.
#                This is the client-facing half of the data guarantee and
#                the only dir-facing data fact the cache will consume.
#   [rd_clean]   by the time the directory reads DRAM for a request, nobody
#                owns the line -- so `mc_owns_img` turns the read into `img`.
#   [wb_img]     what the directory puts on the controller's command wires is
#                accurate to `img`; `mc_owns_img` is proved from it.
#   [wb_done]    an ack is only retired once its data has LANDED in DRAM.
#                This is what lets the client's ownership bookkeeping close.
#
# Deliberately NOT here (phase 5): invalidates, the 8x8 sharer array, SWMR.
# The directory tracks one client, so there is nothing to invalidate; the
# entry array is protocol state for that flow and would carry no invariant
# yet.  `cache_line` IS introduced here rather than in phase 3, so the
# directory is built once in its final shape.
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# PLAN PHASE 5: shared grants, invalidates, and SWMR.
#
# Two caches (8 sets x 2 ways, 2 MSHRs, whole-line data) over German's three
# channels into one home node, over the memory controller, unchanged:
#
#            cache0_body        cache1_body
#          ch1 / ch2 / ch3    ch1 / ch2 / ch3
#                  dir_body                     (grants Shared or Exclusive,
#                four_words beats                invalidates, dirty writebacks)
#                  mc_body                      (UNCHANGED)
#                mem_* top-level wires
#
# What phase 5 adds on top of the exclusive-only directory:
#
#   Permissions.  `tag_exc` beside `tag_occ`/`tag_dty`.  A load hits on
#   presence, a store only on presence AND write permission, and
#   `[dirty_excl]` keeps the dirty bit inside the exclusive one.  A store to
#   a Shared line SELF-DOWNGRADES: it surrenders that clean copy on channel 3
#   through the normal victim path and re-requests the line Exclusive.
#
#   Invalidates.  One held slot per cache, alongside the grant slot rather
#   than sharing it (`gaps.md`: a shared slot brings back the
#   `~(invalidate & grantShared)` mutual-exclusion invariants and buys
#   nothing).  The arm has array and channel-3 priority over everything the
#   cache does, and it answers for a resident copy or for either MSHR's
#   post-grant window -- an invalidate that names a line an MSHR was granted
#   but has not installed bounces that MSHR back to `m_req` with its request
#   still parked, and the reply is clean because a parked store applies only
#   at replay.
#
#   SWMR, per permission.  `[swmr0]`/`[swmr1]`: an exclusive holder excludes
#   every copy in the other cache.  Shared holders coexist -- that is the
#   point -- so the property is no longer "at most one holder" but "at most
#   one WRITER, and no reader beside him".
#
# The directory tracks sharers as one bit per line per CPU plus one exclusive
# bit, and the owner is `dex(H) & dshC(H)` rather than a stored index.  This
# is NOT the plan's 8 sets x 8 ways of entries, and the reason is measured,
# not aesthetic: the sizing argument needs `[d_exact]` in the direction
# "line H has no entry -> H is in no cache", which sends a quantified line
# through `hash` into the entry array and back out through the tag, and
# `ivy_check` rejects that verification condition as outside FAU.  The
# admissible direction quantifies the SET index and derives the line from the
# array -- which requires the holder ghosts themselves to be set-indexed, a
# redesign of the `cachemem` ghost layer.  Deferred, deliberately, exactly as
# the exclusive-only directory deferred it.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: liveness of any kind (an invalidate racing a cache's
# own eviction of the same line can wedge a slot; safety is unaffected), the
# entry array above, and everything in `plan.md`'s non-goals.
# ============================================================================

include numbers
include order

type key
type word
type line
type bit
type bv2
type mshr_id

type store_idx
type load_idx

type four_words

# ---- a whole cache line: the unit the directory and the client trade.
# Two `four_words` beats wide, but the channels never see the seam. ----
type cache_line

function pack4(W3 : word, W2 : word, W1 : word, W0 : word) : four_words
function unp0(M : four_words) : word
function unp1(M : four_words) : word
function unp2(M : four_words) : word
function unp3(M : four_words) : word
function wsel4(M : four_words, J : bv2) : word
function wins4(M : four_words, J : bv2, D : word) : four_words

type addr
type woffset
type way

type cache_slot
# ---- the line <-> beat bridge.  `lsel`/`lins` are the word-granular view a
# cache needs; `lpack`/`lhi`/`llo` are the beat-granular view the memory
# controller needs, and the select-of-pack properties in `data_impl` are the
# only place the two meet. ----
function lsel(L : cache_line, O : woffset) : word
function lins(L : cache_line, O : woffset, D : word) : cache_line
function lpack(HI : four_words, LO : four_words) : cache_line
function lhi(L : cache_line) : four_words
function llo(L : cache_line) : four_words


# ---- the memory controller's service counter.  `nat` has no bit
# width, so it cannot appear in a hardware description. ----
type latency

# ---- control-state encodings.  Ivy enumerated types have no bit width
# either; these are narrow bit vectors whose named values are declared as
# 0-ary wires inside the isolate that uses them. ----
type mstate
type dstate
type wbstate
type mkstate

# Copies of the above functions that can be synthesized to RTL. Kept separate
# so that use does not pull in the adddress theory
wire hw_pack4(W3 : word, W2 : word, W1 : word, W0 : word) : four_words
wire hw_unp0(M : four_words) : word
wire hw_unp1(M : four_words) : word
wire hw_unp2(M : four_words) : word
wire hw_unp3(M : four_words) : word
wire hw_wsel4(M : four_words, J : bv2) : word
wire hw_wins4(M : four_words, J : bv2, D : word) : four_words
wire hw_lsel(L : cache_line, O : woffset) : word
wire hw_lins(L : cache_line, O : woffset, D : word) : cache_line
wire hw_lpack(HI : four_words, LO : four_words) : cache_line
wire hw_lhi(L : cache_line) : four_words
wire hw_llo(L : cache_line) : four_words
wire hw_concatbv3(H : line, O : woffset) : addr
wire hw_high(A : addr) : line
wire hw_low(A : addr) : woffset
wire hw_concatbv2(B : bit, J : bv2) : woffset
wire hw_beat_of(O : woffset) : bit
wire hw_boff2(O : woffset) : bv2
wire hw_hash(H : line) : key
wire hw_cidx(K : key, W : way) : cache_slot

# ---- the narrow index types.  These are interpreted where every
# isolate can see them: they are three and four bit wide, so a
# quantifier over one costs the solver nothing, and both `data_impl`
# and `addr_thy` below need the widths to line up their concatenations.
# ----
isolate basis = {
    interpret key -> bv[3]
    interpret bit -> bv[1]
    interpret bv2 -> bv[2]
    interpret mshr_id -> bv[1]
    interpret store_idx -> bv[3]
    interpret load_idx -> bv[3]
    interpret cache_slot -> bv[4]
    interpret latency -> bv[4]
    interpret mstate -> bv[3]
    interpret dstate -> bv[3]
    interpret wbstate -> bv[3]
    interpret mkstate -> bv[2]
}

function concatbv3(H : line, O : woffset) : addr
function high(A : addr) : line
function low(A : addr) : woffset
function concatbv2(B : bit, J : bv2) : woffset
function beat_of(O : woffset) : bit
function boff2(O : woffset) : bv2
function hash(H : line) : key
function cidx(K : key, W : way) : cache_slot

function init_mem(A : addr) : word   # the power-on memory image

# ---- the same image, indexed by the (line, offset) PAIR.  The cache's
# array and the controller's memory are both powered on from THIS one,
# not from `init_mem`, and that is load-bearing rather than cosmetic:
# `mem_img` below equates the two at an arbitrary address `A`, and if
# one side started at `init_mem(A)` and the other at
# `init_mem(concatbv3(high(A), low(A)))` the base case would need
# `p_recon` -- which `addr_thy` deliberately does not export, because
# outside it that equation is a matching loop.  Starting both from
# `init_img(high(A), low(A))` makes the base case syntactic.
# `p_initimg` ties it back to `init_mem` for the LSQ, which is where
# the trace's own image comes in.
function init_img(H : line, O : woffset) : word

isolate addr_thy = {
    interpret woffset -> bv[3]
    interpret way -> bv[1]
    definition concatbv2(B, J) = B :: J

    property [p_way2]  forall W : way. W = 0 | W = 1
    property [p_way_ne] (0 : way) ~= (1 : way)
    property [p_bit2]  forall B : bit. B = 0 | B = 1
    property [p_boff_beat]  forall B : bit, J : bv2. beat_of(concatbv2(B, J)) = B
    property [p_boff_word]  forall B : bit, J : bv2. boff2(concatbv2(B, J)) = J
    property [p_cover] forall O : woffset, B : bit. beat_of(O) = B ->
        (O = concatbv2(B, 0) | O = concatbv2(B, 1) | O = concatbv2(B, 2) | O = concatbv2(B, 3))
    property [p_full_inj] forall H1 : line, O1 : woffset, H2 : line, O2 : woffset. (concatbv3(H1, O1) = concatbv3(H2, O2)) -> (H1 = H2 & O1 = O2)
    property [p_highfull] forall H : line, O : woffset. high(concatbv3(H, O)) = H
    property [p_lowfull]  forall H : line, O : woffset. low(concatbv3(H, O)) = O

    property [p_hw_high] forall A : addr. hw_high(A) = high(A)
    property [p_hw_low]  forall A : addr. hw_low(A) = low(A)
    property [p_hw_cat3] forall H : line, O : woffset. hw_concatbv3(H, O) = concatbv3(H, O)
    property [p_hw_cat2] forall B : bit, J : bv2. hw_concatbv2(B, J) = concatbv2(B, J)
    property [p_hw_beat] forall O : woffset. hw_beat_of(O) = beat_of(O)
    property [p_hw_boff] forall O : woffset. hw_boff2(O) = boff2(O)
    property [p_hw_hash] forall H : line. hw_hash(H) = hash(H)
    property [p_hw_cidx] forall K : key, W : way. hw_cidx(K, W) = cidx(K, W)

    # ---- the cache slot index.  Unlike `concatbv3`, this one is NOT
    # opaque: `key` and `way` are already interpreted everywhere, the
    # packed slot is four bits wide, and the cache's data invariants
    # quantify over the pair.  Kept abstract, the injectivity they need
    # is an axiom with a two-term trigger that fires on every pair of
    # slots in the query, and `mem_img` -- which carries two such
    # quantifiers and the memory index besides -- stopped coming back.
    # Four bits of concat the solver can just evaluate. ----
    definition cidx(K, W) = K :: W

    # ---- the pair-indexed power-on image agrees with the flat one.
    # This is the ONLY place the two ever have to be compared, and it is
    # exported because the LSQ needs it: the trace's initial state is
    # `init_mem`, the cache's array is `init_img`, and `cimg_ctag` bridges
    # them at time zero. ----
    property [p_initimg] forall H : line, O : woffset.
        init_img(H, O) = init_mem(concatbv3(H, O))

    implementation {
        interpret addr -> bv[64]
        interpret line -> bv[61]
        # (`cache_slot` is interpreted in `basis`, beside `key` and `way`)
        definition high(A) = bfe[3][63](A)
        definition low(A) = bfe[0][2](A)
        definition concatbv3(H, O) = H :: O

        property [p_recon] forall A : addr. concatbv3(high(A), low(A)) = A

        definition hash(H) = bfe[0][2](H)
        definition init_img(H, O) = init_mem(concatbv3(H, O))
        definition beat_of(O) = bfe[2][2](O)
        definition boff2(O)   = bfe[0][1](O)
        definition hw_high(A) = bfe[3][63](A)
        definition hw_low(A)  = bfe[0][2](A)
        definition hw_concatbv3(H, O) = H :: O
        definition hw_concatbv2(B, J) = B :: J
        definition hw_beat_of(O) = bfe[2][2](O)
        definition hw_boff2(O)   = bfe[0][1](O)
        definition hw_hash(H)    = bfe[0][2](H)
        definition hw_cidx(K, W) = K :: W
    }
} with this, basis

# ---- `word`, `four_words` and `cache_line` are ABSTRACT outside this
# isolate.  The bit-level encoding lives in the implementation, so every
# other isolate sees a beat as an uninterpreted quadruple constrained only by
# `p_unp0..3` and a line as an uninterpreted eight-tuple constrained by
# `p_lsel_*`/`p_lins_*`.  This is the same trick `addr_thy` plays above, and
# for the same reason: with four_words = bv[256] visible, every query that
# mentions a fill or a writeback drags a 256-bit concat through the solver,
# and z3 bit-blasts it -- at 512 bits per line it is worse.  Nothing outside
# needs the encoding.
#
# It sits AFTER `addr_thy` and depends on it, which the beat-only version did
# not: the line <-> beat bridge is stated at `concatbv2(B, J)` offsets, so
# this isolate needs `woffset` interpreted and `p_boff_beat`/`p_boff_word` to
# take a packed offset apart.  That is also the plan's "keep the bridge
# axioms confined to the data isolate": they are PROVED here, against the
# encodings, and only their statements escape. ----
isolate data_impl = {
    property [p_unp0] forall W3 : word, W2 : word, W1 : word, W0 : word. unp0(pack4(W3, W2, W1, W0)) = W0
    property [p_unp1] forall W3 : word, W2 : word, W1 : word, W0 : word. unp1(pack4(W3, W2, W1, W0)) = W1
    property [p_unp2] forall W3 : word, W2 : word, W1 : word, W0 : word. unp2(pack4(W3, W2, W1, W0)) = W2
    property [p_unp3] forall W3 : word, W2 : word, W1 : word, W0 : word. unp3(pack4(W3, W2, W1, W0)) = W3

    # ---- the dynamic view of the same four positions.  `bv2` has four
    # values and they are enumerated here, so a caller that knows which
    # word it wants gets the static accessor back and one that does not
    # reasons with `p_wins_hit`/`p_wins_miss` alone. ----
    property [p_wsel0] forall M : four_words. wsel4(M, 0) = unp0(M)
    property [p_wsel1] forall M : four_words. wsel4(M, 1) = unp1(M)
    property [p_wsel2] forall M : four_words. wsel4(M, 2) = unp2(M)
    property [p_wsel3] forall M : four_words. wsel4(M, 3) = unp3(M)
    property [p_wins_hit] forall M : four_words, J : bv2, D : word.
        wsel4(wins4(M, J, D), J) = D
    property [p_wins_miss] forall M : four_words, J1 : bv2, J2 : bv2, D : word.
        J1 ~= J2 -> wsel4(wins4(M, J1, D), J2) = wsel4(M, J2)
    property [p_hw_pack4] forall W3 : word, W2 : word, W1 : word, W0 : word.
        hw_pack4(W3, W2, W1, W0) = pack4(W3, W2, W1, W0)
    property [p_hw_unp0] forall M : four_words. hw_unp0(M) = unp0(M)
    property [p_hw_unp1] forall M : four_words. hw_unp1(M) = unp1(M)
    property [p_hw_unp2] forall M : four_words. hw_unp2(M) = unp2(M)
    property [p_hw_unp3] forall M : four_words. hw_unp3(M) = unp3(M)
    property [p_hw_wsel4] forall M : four_words, J : bv2. hw_wsel4(M, J) = wsel4(M, J)
    property [p_hw_wins4] forall M : four_words, J : bv2, D : word.
        hw_wins4(M, J, D) = wins4(M, J, D)

    # ---- the line <-> beat bridge.  Four facts, and they are all anyone
    # outside ever needs: a packed line reads back as the beats it was built
    # from, and each beat of a line reads back as the line's words at that
    # beat's offsets.  Everything is phrased at `concatbv2(B, J)` so no
    # `beat_of`/`boff2` round trip appears in a client's query. ----
    property [p_lpack_lo] forall HI : four_words, LO : four_words, J : bv2.
        lsel(lpack(HI, LO), concatbv2(0, J)) = wsel4(LO, J)
    property [p_lpack_hi] forall HI : four_words, LO : four_words, J : bv2.
        lsel(lpack(HI, LO), concatbv2(1, J)) = wsel4(HI, J)
    property [p_llo] forall L : cache_line, J : bv2.
        wsel4(llo(L), J) = lsel(L, concatbv2(0, J))
    property [p_lhi] forall L : cache_line, J : bv2.
        wsel4(lhi(L), J) = lsel(L, concatbv2(1, J))
    property [p_lins_hit] forall L : cache_line, O : woffset, D : word.
        lsel(lins(L, O, D), O) = D
    property [p_lins_miss] forall L : cache_line, O1 : woffset, O2 : woffset, D : word.
        O1 ~= O2 -> lsel(lins(L, O1, D), O2) = lsel(L, O2)
    property [p_hw_lsel] forall L : cache_line, O : woffset. hw_lsel(L, O) = lsel(L, O)
    property [p_hw_lins] forall L : cache_line, O : woffset, D : word.
        hw_lins(L, O, D) = lins(L, O, D)
    property [p_hw_lpack] forall HI : four_words, LO : four_words.
        hw_lpack(HI, LO) = lpack(HI, LO)
    property [p_hw_lhi] forall L : cache_line. hw_lhi(L) = lhi(L)
    property [p_hw_llo] forall L : cache_line. hw_llo(L) = llo(L)

    implementation {
        interpret word -> bv[64]
        interpret four_words -> bv[256]
        interpret cache_line -> bv[512]
        definition pack4(W3, W2, W1, W0) = W3 :: W2 :: W1 :: W0
        definition unp0(M) = bfe[0][63](M)
        definition unp1(M) = bfe[64][127](M)
        definition unp2(M) = bfe[128][191](M)
        definition unp3(M) = bfe[192][255](M)
        definition wsel4(M, J) = (unp0(M) if J = 0 else
                                 (unp1(M) if J = 1 else
                                 (unp2(M) if J = 2 else unp3(M))))
        definition wins4(M, J, D) =
            (pack4(unp3(M), unp2(M), unp1(M), D) if J = 0 else
            (pack4(unp3(M), unp2(M), D, unp0(M)) if J = 1 else
            (pack4(unp3(M), D, unp1(M), unp0(M)) if J = 2 else
             pack4(D, unp2(M), unp1(M), unp0(M)))))
        definition hw_pack4(W3, W2, W1, W0) = W3 :: W2 :: W1 :: W0
        definition hw_unp0(M) = bfe[0][63](M)
        definition hw_unp1(M) = bfe[64][127](M)
        definition hw_unp2(M) = bfe[128][191](M)
        definition hw_unp3(M) = bfe[192][255](M)
        definition hw_wsel4(M, J) = (hw_unp0(M) if J = 0 else
                                    (hw_unp1(M) if J = 1 else
                                    (hw_unp2(M) if J = 2 else hw_unp3(M))))
        definition hw_wins4(M, J, D) =
            (hw_pack4(hw_unp3(M), hw_unp2(M), hw_unp1(M), D) if J = 0 else
            (hw_pack4(hw_unp3(M), hw_unp2(M), D, hw_unp0(M)) if J = 1 else
            (hw_pack4(hw_unp3(M), D, hw_unp1(M), hw_unp0(M)) if J = 2 else
             hw_pack4(D, hw_unp2(M), hw_unp1(M), hw_unp0(M)))))

        # ---- a line is its two beats, high first.  Every line operation is
        # routed through `wsel4`/`wins4` on the selected half rather than
        # through a fresh 512-bit case split, which is what keeps the six
        # properties above provable in seconds. ----
        definition lpack(HI, LO) = HI :: LO
        definition llo(L) = bfe[0][255](L)
        definition lhi(L) = bfe[256][511](L)
        definition lsel(L, O) = (wsel4(llo(L), boff2(O)) if beat_of(O) = 0
                                 else wsel4(lhi(L), boff2(O)))
        definition lins(L, O, D) =
            (lpack(lhi(L), wins4(llo(L), boff2(O), D)) if beat_of(O) = 0
             else lpack(wins4(lhi(L), boff2(O), D), llo(L)))
        definition hw_lpack(HI, LO) = HI :: LO
        definition hw_llo(L) = bfe[0][255](L)
        definition hw_lhi(L) = bfe[256][511](L)
        definition hw_lsel(L, O) = (hw_wsel4(hw_llo(L), hw_boff2(O)) if hw_beat_of(O) = 0
                                    else hw_wsel4(hw_lhi(L), hw_boff2(O)))
        definition hw_lins(L, O, D) =
            (hw_lpack(hw_lhi(L), hw_wins4(hw_llo(L), hw_boff2(O), D)) if hw_beat_of(O) = 0
             else hw_lpack(hw_wins4(hw_lhi(L), hw_boff2(O), D), hw_llo(L)))
    }
} with this, basis, addr_thy

export action posedge



    isolate cachemem = {

        # ---- the memory-side port, carried out to the top of the design ----
        wire mem_rd_en   : bool
        wire mem_rd_addr : addr
        wire mem_wr_en   : bool
        wire mem_wr_addr : addr
        wire mem_wr_data : four_words
        wire mem_rdata   : four_words

        # ---- one LSQ channel per CPU ----
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_en0     : bool
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_h0      : line
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_o0      : woffset
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_wen0    : bool
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_wdata0  : word
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_idx0    : load_idx
        wire cache_to_lsq_creq_ready0 : bool
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0 : bool
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_h0     : line
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_o0     : woffset
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_data0  : word
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_idx0   : load_idx
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_en1     : bool
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_h1      : line
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_o1      : woffset
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_wen1    : bool
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_wdata1  : word
        wire lsq_to_cache_req_idx1    : load_idx
        wire cache_to_lsq_creq_ready1 : bool
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1 : bool
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_h1     : line
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_o1     : woffset
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_data1  : word
        wire cache_to_lsq_resp_idx1   : load_idx

        # ==================================================================
        # Two coherence channel sets, one per CPU, declared ONCE here ahead
        # of all three children.  Each net has exactly one name: the child
        # that drives it exports a port wire and this level aliases the net
        # to it, and the child that reads it reads THIS wire.
        #
        # That is not tidiness.  `ivy_check` admits a definition into an
        # isolate's query only when the defined symbol occurs in that
        # isolate's OWN code or invariants -- an occurrence inside an
        # invariant imported from a sibling does not count.  With a net named
        # twice, an imported invariant about the far end's copy is a
        # statement about a symbol the query never connects to anything, and
        # it silently says nothing.  (Measured: the query does not fail, it
        # runs for twenty minutes.)
        # ==================================================================
        wire c0_req_en    : bool
        wire c0_req_line  : line
        wire c0_req_excl  : bool
        wire c0_req_taken : bool
        wire c0_gr_valid  : bool
        wire c0_gr_excl   : bool
        wire c0_gr_line   : line
        wire c0_gr_data   : cache_line
        wire c0_gr_taken  : bool
        wire c0_ack_en    : bool
        wire c0_ack_line  : line
        wire c0_ack_dty   : bool
        wire c0_ack_dat   : cache_line
        wire c0_inv_valid : bool
        wire c0_inv_line  : line
        wire c0_inv_taken : bool
        wire c0_ack_taken : bool
        wire c0_st_en     : bool
        wire c0_st_h      : line
        wire c0_st_o      : woffset
        wire c0_st_d      : word
        # 
        wire c0_give_en   : bool
        wire c0_give_line : line
        wire c0_give_dty  : bool
        wire c0_give_dat  : cache_line
        wire c1_req_en    : bool
        wire c1_req_line  : line
        wire c1_req_excl  : bool
        wire c1_req_taken : bool
        wire c1_gr_valid  : bool
        wire c1_gr_excl   : bool
        wire c1_gr_line   : line
        wire c1_gr_data   : cache_line
        wire c1_gr_taken  : bool
        wire c1_ack_en    : bool
        wire c1_ack_line  : line
        wire c1_ack_dty   : bool
        wire c1_ack_dat   : cache_line
        wire c1_inv_valid : bool
        wire c1_inv_line  : line
        wire c1_inv_taken : bool
        wire c1_ack_taken : bool
        wire c1_st_en     : bool
        wire c1_st_h      : line
        wire c1_st_o      : woffset
        wire c1_st_d      : word
        wire c1_give_en   : bool
        wire c1_give_line : line
        wire c1_give_dty  : bool
        wire c1_give_dat  : cache_line

        specification {

            # ---- the ONE memory the two caches jointly present ----
            function img(H : line, O : woffset) : word

            # ---- write debt, split per CPU: `dirtiedC(H,O)` says cache C
            # committed a store to word (H,O) that DRAM has not yet
            # absorbed -- set at the store commit, cleared when the
            # writeback beat lands.  NOT permission (that is `exclusiveC`)
            # and not possession (`holdsC`): exclusive-clean lines exist,
            # and a dirtied word outlives its line's eviction while the ack
            # is in flight.  Each half is that cache's part of the old
            # `[unowned]` and has to be provable with no protocol
            # assumptions at all -- that is what keeps the rely-guarantee
            # chain acyclic (the directory consumes these to return SWMR,
            # so they must not depend on SWMR).  The controller needs both
            # halves clear before it can claim DRAM is the image. ----
            function dirtied0(H : line, O : woffset) : bool
            function dirtied1(H : line, O : woffset) : bool

            # ==============================================================
            # What each cache holds, and its channel 3, kept HERE.
            #
            # Not decoration: an isolate cannot prove anything about its own
            # free inputs.  The directory has to state that the writeback it
            # is running is the ack still in the channel, and that a retired
            # ack's data has landed -- both are claims about the POST-state,
            # and if the ack lived in a cache's registers then across the
            # seam it would be a wire the environment may re-drive.
            # (Measured: the query does not fail, it diverges.)
            # ==============================================================
            function holds0(H : line) : bool
            function holds1(H : line) : bool
            # ---- permission is tracked beside possession.  Shared copies
            # may coexist; an exclusive copy excludes every other holder. ----
            function exclusive0(H : line) : bool
            function exclusive1(H : line) : bool

            var ack0_g_en   : bool
            var ack0_g_line : line
            var ack0_g_dty  : bool
            var ack0_g_data : cache_line
            var ack1_g_en   : bool
            var ack1_g_line : line
            var ack1_g_dty  : bool
            var ack1_g_data : cache_line

            after init {
                img(H, O)     := init_img(H, O);
                dirtied0(H, O)   := false;
                dirtied1(H, O)   := false;
                holds0(H)     := false;
                holds1(H)     := false;
                exclusive0(H) := false;
                exclusive1(H) := false;
                ack0_g_en := false; ack0_g_line := 0;
                ack0_g_dty := false; ack0_g_data := 0;
                ack1_g_en := false; ack1_g_line := 0;
                ack1_g_dty := false; ack1_g_data := 0;
            }

            after posedge {

                # ---- CLEAR dirtied words at a command-beat landing, BEFORE the
                # store arms below.  BOTH halves clear: the beat that lands
                # is accurate to `img` (`[wb_img]`), so after it DRAM agrees
                # with the image at those words no matter which cache had
                # been holding them. ----
                if mc_body.cmd_en & mc_body.cmd_wen {
                    dirtied0(H, O) := (false
                                    if (H = high(mc_body.cmd_addr)
                                        & beat_of(O) = beat_of(low(mc_body.cmd_addr)))
                                    else dirtied0(H, O));
                    dirtied1(H, O) := (false
                                    if (H = high(mc_body.cmd_addr)
                                        & beat_of(O) = beat_of(low(mc_body.cmd_addr)))
                                    else dirtied1(H, O));
                };

                # ---- a load answer is BINDING on the image.  With
                # `[resp_img_int_*]` these arms rewrite `img` with the value
                # it already holds, so they are no-ops today; they are here
                # so the weak contract is the one the machine is proved
                # against.  AHEAD of the store arms: a commit on this edge
                # must not be rolled back by an answer that predates it. ----
                if cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0 {
                    img(H, O) := (cache_to_lsq_resp_data0
                                  if (H = cache_to_lsq_resp_h0
                                      & O = cache_to_lsq_resp_o0)
                                  else img(H, O));
                };
                if cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1 {
                    img(H, O) := (cache_to_lsq_resp_data1
                                  if (H = cache_to_lsq_resp_h1
                                      & O = cache_to_lsq_resp_o1)
                                  else img(H, O));
                };

                # ---- the store commits.  `img` moves HERE and nowhere else.
                # Both CPUs can commit on one edge, but only to different
                # lines -- that is the exclusivity backbone, and it is why
                # the two arms compose. ----
                if c0_st_en {
                    img(H, O) := (c0_st_d
                                  if (H = c0_st_h & O = c0_st_o)
                                  else img(H, O));
                    dirtied0(H, O) := (true
                                    if (H = c0_st_h & O = c0_st_o)
                                    else dirtied0(H, O));
                };
                if c1_st_en {
                    img(H, O) := (c1_st_d
                                  if (H = c1_st_h & O = c1_st_o)
                                  else img(H, O));
                    dirtied1(H, O) := (true
                                    if (H = c1_st_h & O = c1_st_o)
                                    else dirtied1(H, O));
                };

                # ---- channel 3, per CPU: retire first, post second ----
                if c0_ack_taken { ack0_g_en := false; };
                if c0_give_en {
                    ack0_g_en   := true;
                    ack0_g_line := c0_give_line;
                    ack0_g_dty  := c0_give_dty;
                    ack0_g_data := c0_give_dat;
                };
                if c1_ack_taken { ack1_g_en := false; };
                if c1_give_en {
                    ack1_g_en   := true;
                    ack1_g_line := c1_give_line;
                    ack1_g_dty  := c1_give_dty;
                    ack1_g_data := c1_give_dat;
                };

                # ---- possession and permission follow accepted grants and
                # every channel-3 surrender, including invalidate replies. ----
                if c0_give_en {
                    holds0(H) := (false if H = c0_give_line else holds0(H));
                    exclusive0(H) := (false if H = c0_give_line else exclusive0(H));
                };
                if c0_gr_valid & c0_gr_taken {
                    holds0(H) := (true if H = c0_gr_line else holds0(H));
                    exclusive0(H) := (c0_gr_excl if H = c0_gr_line else exclusive0(H));
                };
                if c1_give_en {
                    holds1(H) := (false if H = c1_give_line else holds1(H));
                    exclusive1(H) := (false if H = c1_give_line else exclusive1(H));
                };
                if c1_gr_valid & c1_gr_taken {
                    holds1(H) := (true if H = c1_gr_line else holds1(H));
                    exclusive1(H) := (c1_gr_excl if H = c1_gr_line else exclusive1(H));
                };
            }
        }
        # ==================================================================
        # CPU 0's cache.  8 sets x 2 ways, 2 MSHRs, whole-line data.
        #
        # EVERY eviction is non-silent (plan decision 1): clean or dirty,
        # the victim goes out on channel 3, so the directory's sharer bit is
        # exact and it never has to recall a line it has lost track of.  A
        # give-up therefore cannot start until the channel-3 slot is free.
        # ==================================================================
        isolate cache0_body = {

            # ---- LSQ request channel: the request comes from the LSQ, the
            # ready is driven here.  `lsq_to_cache_req_idx0` is opaque to the
            # cache: it is stored beside the request in the MSHR slot and
            # handed straight back on the response. ----
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_en0     : bool
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_h0      : line
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_o0      : woffset
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_wen0    : bool
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_wdata0  : word
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_idx0    : load_idx
            wire cache_to_lsq_creq_ready0 : bool

            # ---- LSQ response channel: all driven here ----
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0 : bool
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_h0     : line
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_o0     : woffset
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_data0  : word
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_idx0   : load_idx

            # ---- the ports this cache drives on the directory side ----
            wire req_en    : bool
            wire req_line  : line
            wire req_excl  : bool
            wire gr_taken  : bool
            wire inv_taken : bool

            # ---- channel 3 as REAL hardware, held by its producer (plan
            # decision 2).  `cachemem` keeps a ghost copy of the same slot --
            # the directory's `[wb_*]` invariants are claims about the
            # POST-state and need a symbol the environment cannot re-drive --
            # and `[ack_sync_*]` pins the two together, the `ae_r` pattern
            # widened from the enable bit to the whole payload.  Without these
            # registers the directory's datapath reads specification state and
            # `ivy_to_rtl` emits a dangling wire. ----
            wire ack_en    : bool
            wire ack_line  : line
            wire ack_dty   : bool
            wire ack_dat   : cache_line

            # ---- the pending-store view the LSQ contract is stated over.
            # A store is accepted at the port one edge before it reaches the
            # array: it waits in the stage, and on a miss it waits again in
            # the allocating MSHR's slot.  Those three records ARE
            # `in_progress`, and they are exported as wires so the contract
            # can name them without naming the registers behind them. ----
            wire stage_go : bool
            wire ps_v : bool
            wire ps_h : line
            wire ps_o : woffset
            wire ps_d : word
            wire p0_v : bool
            wire p0_h : line
            wire p0_o : woffset
            wire p0_d : word
            wire p1_v : bool
            wire p1_h : line
            wire p1_o : woffset
            wire p1_d : word
            wire in_progress(H : line) : bool

            # ---- proof observation ports ----
            wire st_en     : bool
            wire st_h      : line
            wire st_o      : woffset
            wire st_d      : word
            wire give_en   : bool
            wire give_line : line
            wire give_dty  : bool
            wire give_dat  : cache_line

            # ==============================================================
            # The contract.
            #
            # `[resp_img]` is the LSQ-facing half: the datum handed back is
            # the image at the address the response echoes.  Responses always
            # come out of the array and `[c_img_p0]` pins the array to `img`, so
            # it holds unconditionally -- phase 4 restates it weakly, over
            # `in_progress`, without the implementation changing.
            #
            # The rest is the directory-facing half, and it is the same seven
            # facts the phase-2 stand-in exported, generalised from one held
            # line to a set: a line under request is not held and has no ack
            # outstanding; a store only ever lands in a held line; a give-up
            # does not collide with a live ack or with a store; and -- the
            # cache's half of the old `[unowned]` -- a dirtied word lives in
            # a held line or in the dirty ack carrying it.
            # ==============================================================
            specification {
                # TODO: why does this stronger invariant hold?
                invariant [resp_img_int_p0] cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0 ->
                    cache_to_lsq_resp_data0 = img(cache_to_lsq_resp_h0,
                                                 cache_to_lsq_resp_o0)

                # the data we give to the LSQ is valid. 
                invariant [resp_quiet_p0] cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0
                    & ~in_progress(cache_to_lsq_resp_h0) ->
                    cache_to_lsq_resp_data0 = img(cache_to_lsq_resp_h0,
                                                 cache_to_lsq_resp_o0)

                invariant [resp_inprog_p0] cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0
                    & in_progress(cache_to_lsq_resp_h0) ->
                      cache_to_lsq_resp_data0 = img(cache_to_lsq_resp_h0,
                                                   cache_to_lsq_resp_o0)
                    | (ps_v & ps_h = cache_to_lsq_resp_h0
                       & ps_o = cache_to_lsq_resp_o0
                       & cache_to_lsq_resp_data0 = ps_d)
                    | (p0_v & p0_h = cache_to_lsq_resp_h0
                       & p0_o = cache_to_lsq_resp_o0
                       & cache_to_lsq_resp_data0 = p0_d)
                    | (p1_v & p1_h = cache_to_lsq_resp_h0
                       & p1_o = cache_to_lsq_resp_o0
                       & cache_to_lsq_resp_data0 = p1_d)

                
                # The cache only surrenders lines it holds
                invariant [ca_gv_hold_p0] c0_give_en -> holds0(c0_give_line)
                # Guarantee to the directory that if we don't hold, then
                # not exclusive
                invariant [ca_excl_hold_p0] exclusive0(H) -> holds0(H)

                # Storing a line implies that we have exclusive perms
                invariant [ca_st_excl_p0] c0_st_en -> exclusive0(c0_st_h)

                # Cache only takes the invalidate line if it has a line to invalidate
                invariant [ca_inv_reply_p0] c0_inv_taken ->
                    c0_give_en & c0_give_line = c0_inv_line

                invariant [ca_gv_nodouble_p0] c0_give_en -> ~ack0_g_en

                invariant [ca_gv_nost_p0] c0_give_en -> ~c0_st_en

                # A clean surrender carries no write debt: no word of the
                # line is dirtied, so the directory may keep trusting its
                # own copy of it.
                invariant [ca_gv_clean_p0] c0_give_en & ~c0_give_dty ->
                    ~dirtied0(c0_give_line, O)
                # A dirty surrender is believable as the whole image only
                # under SWMR: dirty implies the copy was Exclusive.
                invariant [ca_gv_dirty_excl_p0] c0_give_en & c0_give_dty ->
                    exclusive0(c0_give_line)

                # Write debt never floats unaccounted: a word is dirtied
                # only while its line is held or while the dirty ack
                # carrying it is in flight.
                invariant [ca_dirtied_p0]
                      ~holds0(H)
                    & ~(ack0_g_en & ack0_g_dty & ack0_g_line = H)
                    -> ~dirtied0(H, O)
                invariant [ca_dirtied_excl_p0] dirtied0(H, O) ->
                      exclusive0(H)
                    | (ack0_g_en & ack0_g_dty & ack0_g_line = H)

                # A clean ack vouches for nothing: no word of its line is dirtied.
                invariant [ca_clean_ack_p0] ack0_g_en & ~ack0_g_dty ->
                    ~dirtied0(ack0_g_line, O)

                # If we send an invalidate acknowledgement, then we don't hold the line
                invariant [ca_ack_idle_p0] ack0_g_en -> ~holds0(ack0_g_line)

                # Data we send as part of the invalidate is valid
                # TODO: how does this work w/ consistency model?
                invariant [ack_img_p0] ack0_g_en & ack0_g_dty ->
                    lsel(ack0_g_data, O) = img(ack0_g_line, O)

                # ---- the hardware slot and the ghost slot are the same slot ----
                invariant [ack_sync_en_p0] c0_ack_en = ack0_g_en
                invariant [ack_sync_line_p0] ack0_g_en ->
                    c0_ack_line = ack0_g_line
                invariant [ack_sync_dty_p0] ack0_g_en ->
                    c0_ack_dty = ack0_g_dty
                invariant [ack_sync_dat_p0] ack0_g_en ->
                    c0_ack_dat = ack0_g_data
            }

            implementation {
                # ==========================================================
                # The array.  One memory per former struct field, indexed by
                # the packed (set, way) slot -- an RTLIL memory is
                # one-dimensional and a struct sort has no bit width.  The
                # data is ONE whole line per slot now: a fill writes it in a
                # single edge, so there is nothing left for the beat split to
                # buy.
                #
                # `set_lru` is ONE bit per set, not one per way: per way it
                # took two writes to the same memory on every hit, which the
                # translator merges into a single port.
                # ==========================================================
                # `tag_exc` is written at a fill, off the grant's kind, and
                # RE-ASSERTED on the two store paths.  The re-assert is
                # provably idempotent -- a store only commits where the
                # permission already is -- but stating it syntactically is
                # what keeps `[ca_gv_clean_*]` inductive: without it the
                # solver has to rediscover the permission through the hit
                # test, and the query stops coming back.
                var tag_occ(S : cache_slot) : bool
                var tag_dty(S : cache_slot) : bool
                var tag_exc(S : cache_slot) : bool
                var tag_ln(S : cache_slot)  : line
                var set_lru(K : key)        : bit
                var dat(S : cache_slot)     : cache_line

                # ---- the (set, way) view the invariants are written in.
                # Plain defined functions, so `ivy_to_rtl` never sees them. ----
                function occupied(K : key, W : way) : bool
                definition occupied(K, W) = tag_occ(cidx(K, W))
                function dirty(K : key, W : way) : bool
                definition dirty(K, W) = tag_dty(cidx(K, W))
                function exclusive(K : key, W : way) : bool
                definition exclusive(K, W) = tag_exc(cidx(K, W))
                function ltag(K : key, W : way) : line
                definition ltag(K, W) = tag_ln(cidx(K, W))
                function val(K : key, W : way, O : woffset) : word
                definition val(K, W, O) = lsel(dat(cidx(K, W)), O)

                # ---- the MSHR's control states.  An enumeration has no bit
                # width, so these are narrow bit vectors and the names are
                # 0-ary wires. ----
                wire m_idle   : mstate
                wire m_req    : mstate
                wire m_wait   : mstate
                wire m_fill   : mstate
                wire m_replay : mstate
                definition m_idle   = 0
                definition m_req    = 1
                definition m_wait   = 2
                definition m_fill   = 3
                definition m_replay = 4

                # ==========================================================
                # The two MSHRs, as registers rather than as an array over
                # `mshr_id`: `m_state` is written by the capture, by an
                # allocation and by a fill on the same edge, and a memory has
                # one write port per clock.  The proof sees them only at the
                # two literal indices, which is what keeps every invariant a
                # clean trigger.
                #
                # `mvline` is GHOST: the line this MSHR is evicting, recorded
                # when the victim way is picked.  Metadata carried BESIDE the
                # slot rather than read back out of it, which is what keeps
                # the writeback proof inside the decidable fragment -- naming
                # the victim as `ltag(hash(m_base(Id)), ...)` under a
                # quantifier sends the query from a line, through `hash`,
                # into the array and back out to a line.
                # ==========================================================
                var s0_m_state : mstate
                var s0_m_base  : line
                var s0_m_excl  : bool
                var s0_m_vway  : way
                var s0_mvline  : line
                var s0_mfb     : cache_line
                var s0_rq_valid : bool
                var s0_rq_off   : woffset
                var s0_rq_wen   : bool
                var s0_rq_wdata : word
                var s0_rq_idx   : load_idx
                var s1_m_state : mstate
                var s1_m_base  : line
                var s1_m_excl  : bool
                var s1_m_vway  : way
                var s1_mvline  : line
                var s1_mfb     : cache_line
                var s1_rq_valid : bool
                var s1_rq_off   : woffset
                var s1_rq_wen   : bool
                var s1_rq_wdata : word
                var s1_rq_idx   : load_idx

                # ---- the input stage.  ALL requests pass through it. ----
                var p_valid : bool
                var p_h     : line
                var p_o     : woffset
                var p_wen   : bool
                var p_wdata : word
                var p_idx   : load_idx

                var port_used : bool

                var ev_ans     : bool
                var ev_ans_h   : line
                var ev_ans_o   : woffset
                var ev_ans_d   : word
                var ev_ans_idx : load_idx

                # ---- this side's copy of the channel-3 slot.  A guard has
                # to be a function of THIS isolate's registers: the record at
                # `cachemem` is written by a monitor that runs before this
                # one, so a guard phrased over `ack0_g_en` would be read there
                # at its old value and re-read here at its new one. ----
                var ae_r : bool
                var ae_line : line
                var ae_dty  : bool
                var ae_dat  : cache_line

                after init {
                    tag_occ(S) := false; tag_dty(S) := false; tag_exc(S) := false; tag_ln(S) := 0;
                    set_lru(K) := 0; dat(S) := 0;
                    s0_m_state := 0; s0_m_base := 0; s0_m_excl := false;
                    s0_m_vway := 0; s0_mvline := 0; s0_mfb := 0;
                    s0_rq_valid := false; s0_rq_off := 0; s0_rq_wen := false;
                    s0_rq_wdata := 0; s0_rq_idx := 0;
                    s1_m_state := 0; s1_m_base := 0; s1_m_excl := false;
                    s1_m_vway := 0; s1_mvline := 0; s1_mfb := 0;
                    s1_rq_valid := false; s1_rq_off := 0; s1_rq_wen := false;
                    s1_rq_wdata := 0; s1_rq_idx := 0;
                    p_valid := false; p_h := 0; p_o := 0; p_wen := false;
                    p_wdata := 0; p_idx := 0;
                    port_used := false;
                    ev_ans := false; ev_ans_h := 0; ev_ans_o := 0;
                    ev_ans_d := 0; ev_ans_idx := 0;
                    ae_r := false;
                    ae_line := 0; ae_dty := false; ae_dat := 0;
                }

                # ==========================================================
                # The arbiter, written out as three wires.  `stage_go` is the
                # stage-movable gate: the staged request enters only when its
                # line aliases no active MSHR, no MSHR wants the array this
                # edge, and an MSHR is free to take a miss.  Otherwise the
                # first MSHR that wants the array gets it.  Exactly one of
                # the three is true, which is what makes the single-writer
                # discipline on the array and on `c_st_*` syntactic.
                #
                # The gate does NOT mention the response port.  It does not
                # have to: one array action per edge means a store commit and
                # a load answer never share an edge, so `[resp_img]` cannot
                # be overtaken by an image move.
                # ==========================================================
                # ---- presence and permission are separate.  Loads hit any
                # resident copy; stores hit only an Exclusive copy. ----
                wire p_present : bool
                wire p_hit     : bool
                wire p_hway    : way
                wire p_hexcl   : bool
                wire p_slot    : cache_slot
                definition p_present =
                      (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 1)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 1)) = p_h)
                    | (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 0)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 0)) = p_h)
                definition p_hway =
                    (1 if (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 1))
                           & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 1)) = p_h) else 0)
                definition p_slot = hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), p_hway)
                definition p_hexcl = p_present & tag_exc(p_slot)
                definition p_hit = p_present & (~p_wen | p_hexcl)
                wire arb0 : bool
                wire arb1 : bool

                # ---- invalidates use a separate held slot and have array and
                # channel-3 priority.  An invalidate is answered for a
                # resident copy AND for either MSHR's post-grant window,
                # where the line is held but not yet installed: that MSHR
                # bounces back to `m_req` with its request still parked, and
                # the reply is clean because the parked store applies only at
                # replay.  At most one of the three can match: `[c_uniq]`
                # keeps a line in one way and `[mshr_distinct_p0]` keeps the
                # two MSHRs off each other's line. ----
                wire inv_hit    : bool
                wire inv_hway   : way
                wire inv_slot   : cache_slot
                wire inv_mshr0  : bool
                wire inv_mshr1  : bool
                wire inv_act    : bool
                definition inv_hit =
                      (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c0_inv_line), 1)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c0_inv_line), 1)) = c0_inv_line)
                    | (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c0_inv_line), 0)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c0_inv_line), 0)) = c0_inv_line)
                definition inv_hway =
                    (1 if (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c0_inv_line), 1))
                           & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c0_inv_line), 1)) = c0_inv_line) else 0)
                definition inv_slot = hw_cidx(hw_hash(c0_inv_line), inv_hway)
                definition inv_mshr0 = (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                    & s0_m_base = c0_inv_line
                definition inv_mshr1 = (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                    & s1_m_base = c0_inv_line
                definition inv_act = c0_inv_valid & (inv_hit | inv_mshr0 | inv_mshr1)
                definition inv_taken = inv_act & ~ae_r

                # ---- the stage-movable gate, back to its two-MSHR shape:
                # the staged request enters only when its line aliases no
                # active MSHR, no MSHR wants the array this edge, and an MSHR
                # is free to take a miss -- plus, new here, channel 3 is free
                # if the request is a store that has to self-downgrade a
                # Shared copy first.  Otherwise the first MSHR that wants the
                # array gets it, and an invalidate outranks all of them. ----
                definition stage_go = p_valid
                    & ~inv_act
                    & ~(s0_m_state ~= m_idle & s0_m_base = p_h)
                    & ~(s1_m_state ~= m_idle & s1_m_base = p_h)
                    & ~(s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                    & ~(s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                    & (s0_m_state = m_idle | s1_m_state = m_idle)
                    & (~p_wen | ~p_present | p_hexcl | ~ae_r)
                definition arb0 = ~stage_go & ~inv_act
                    & (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                definition arb1 = ~stage_go & ~inv_act
                    & ~(s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                    & (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)

                definition ps_v = p_valid & p_wen
                definition ps_h = p_h
                definition ps_o = p_o
                definition ps_d = p_wdata
                definition p0_v = s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen
                definition p0_h = s0_m_base
                definition p0_o = s0_rq_off
                definition p0_d = s0_rq_wdata
                definition p1_v = s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen
                definition p1_h = s1_m_base
                definition p1_o = s1_rq_off
                definition p1_d = s1_rq_wdata
                definition in_progress(H) =
                      (p_valid & p_wen & p_h = H)
                    | (s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen & s0_m_base = H)
                    | (s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen & s1_m_base = H)

                # ---- the victim slot each MSHR is aiming at ----
                wire v0 : cache_slot
                wire v1 : cache_slot
                definition v0 = hw_cidx(hw_hash(s0_m_base), s0_m_vway)
                definition v1 = hw_cidx(hw_hash(s1_m_base), s1_m_vway)

                # ---- channel 3 has four producers now: the invalidate
                # reply, the store's self-downgrade, and each MSHR's
                # fill-eviction.  They are one-hot because the invalidate
                # outranks everything and `stage_go`, `arb0`, `arb1` are
                # mutually exclusive. ----
                wire gv0 : bool
                wire gv1 : bool
                wire ugv : bool
                definition gv0 = (s0_m_state = m_fill) & tag_occ(v0) & ~ae_r
                definition gv1 = (s1_m_state = m_fill) & tag_occ(v1) & ~ae_r
                definition ugv = stage_go & p_wen & p_present & ~p_hexcl

                definition give_en = inv_taken | ugv | (arb0 & gv0) | (arb1 & gv1)
                definition give_line = (c0_inv_line if inv_taken else
                                        (p_h if ugv else
                                         (s0_mvline if arb0 else s1_mvline)))
                definition give_dty = ((tag_dty(inv_slot) if inv_hit else false) if inv_taken else
                                       (tag_dty(p_slot) if ugv else
                                        (tag_dty(v0) if arb0 else tag_dty(v1))))
                definition give_dat = ((dat(inv_slot) if inv_hit else 0) if inv_taken else
                                       (dat(p_slot) if ugv else
                                        (dat(v0) if arb0 else dat(v1))))

                # ---- a store commits on the stage's Exclusive-hit path or on
                # an MSHR's replay ----
                definition st_en = (stage_go & p_wen & p_hit)
                    | (arb0 & s0_m_state = m_replay & s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen)
                    | (arb1 & s1_m_state = m_replay & s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen)
                definition st_h = (p_h if stage_go
                                   else (s0_m_base if arb0 else s1_m_base))
                definition st_o = (p_o if stage_go
                                   else (s0_rq_off if arb0 else s1_rq_off))
                definition st_d = (p_wdata if stage_go
                                   else (s0_rq_wdata if arb0 else s1_rq_wdata))

                # ---- channel 1 is held by one `m_req` MSHR at a time, MSHR0
                # first ----
                definition req_en   = (s0_m_state = m_req) | (s1_m_state = m_req)
                definition req_line = (s0_m_base if s0_m_state = m_req
                                       else s1_m_base)
                definition req_excl = (s0_m_excl if s0_m_state = m_req
                                       else s1_m_excl)

                # ---- a grant is consumed the edge it matches a waiting
                # MSHR, and only if its kind covers what that MSHR parked:
                # `gr_line = m_base` is unambiguous because the two MSHRs
                # never fetch the same line (`[mshr_distinct_p0]`) ----
                definition gr_taken = c0_gr_valid & ~c0_inv_valid
                    & ((s0_m_state = m_wait & s0_m_base = c0_gr_line
                        & (~s0_rq_wen | c0_gr_excl))
                       | (s1_m_state = m_wait & s1_m_base = c0_gr_line
                          & (~s1_rq_wen | c0_gr_excl)))

                definition ack_en   = ae_r
                definition ack_line = ae_line
                definition ack_dty  = ae_dty
                definition ack_dat  = ae_dat

                definition cache_to_lsq_creq_ready0 = ~p_valid | stage_go

                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0 = ev_ans
                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_h0     = ev_ans_h
                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_o0     = ev_ans_o
                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_data0  = ev_ans_d
                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_idx0   = ev_ans_idx

                # ==========================================================
                # The three array actions.
                # ==========================================================
                action serve_stage = {
                    var h  : line    := p_h;
                    var o  : woffset := p_o;
                    var rs : key     := hw_hash(h);
                    var present : bool := false;
                    var hit     : bool := false;
                    var hway    : way  := 1;
                    p_valid := false;
                    if tag_occ(hw_cidx(rs, 1)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(rs, 1)) = h {
                        present := true; hway := 1;
                    } else { if tag_occ(hw_cidx(rs, 0)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(rs, 0)) = h {
                        present := true; hway := 0;
                    } };
                    hit := present & (~p_wen | tag_exc(hw_cidx(rs, hway)));
                    if hit {
                        if p_wen {
                            dat(hw_cidx(rs, hway)) :=
                                hw_lins(dat(hw_cidx(rs, hway)), o, p_wdata);
                            tag_dty(hw_cidx(rs, hway)) := true;
                            tag_exc(hw_cidx(rs, hway)) := true;
                        } else {
                            ev_ans     := true;
                            ev_ans_h   := h;
                            ev_ans_o   := o;
                            ev_ans_d   := hw_lsel(dat(hw_cidx(rs, hway)), o);
                            ev_ans_idx := p_idx;
                            port_used  := true;
                        };
                        if hway = 1 { set_lru(rs) := 0; } else { set_lru(rs) := 1; };
                    } else {
                        # A store to Shared surrenders that clean copy before
                        # requesting Exclusive.  `stage_go` waited for ch3.
                        if present {
                            ae_r := true;
                            ae_line := h;
                            ae_dty  := tag_dty(hw_cidx(rs, hway));
                            ae_dat  := dat(hw_cidx(rs, hway));
                            tag_occ(hw_cidx(rs, hway)) := false;
                        };
                        # ---- allocate.  Under the gate one MSHR is idle, so
                        # the else arm is closed by construction. ----
                        if s0_m_state = m_idle {
                            s0_m_state := m_req;
                            s0_m_base  := h;
                            s0_m_excl  := p_wen;
                            s0_rq_valid := true; s0_rq_off := o;
                            s0_rq_wen := p_wen; s0_rq_wdata := p_wdata;
                            s0_rq_idx := p_idx;
                        } else {
                            s1_m_state := m_req;
                            s1_m_base  := h;
                            s1_m_excl  := p_wen;
                            s1_rq_valid := true; s1_rq_off := o;
                            s1_rq_wen := p_wen; s1_rq_wdata := p_wdata;
                            s1_rq_idx := p_idx;
                        };
                    };
                }

                action step_0(ae0 : bool) = {
                    if s0_m_state = m_fill {
                        if tag_occ(v0) {
                            if ~ae0 {
                                ae_r := true;
                                ae_line := s0_mvline;
                                ae_dty  := tag_dty(v0);
                                ae_dat  := dat(v0);
                                tag_occ(v0) := false;
                            };
                        } else {
                            dat(v0)     := s0_mfb;
                            tag_ln(v0)  := s0_m_base;
                            tag_dty(v0) := false;
                            tag_exc(v0) := s0_m_excl;
                            tag_occ(v0) := true;
                            if s0_m_vway = 1 { set_lru(hw_hash(s0_m_base)) := 0; }
                            else { set_lru(hw_hash(s0_m_base)) := 1; };
                            s0_m_state := m_replay;
                        };
                    } else {
                        if s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen {
                            dat(v0) := hw_lins(dat(v0), s0_rq_off, s0_rq_wdata);
                            tag_dty(v0) := true;
                            tag_exc(v0) := true;
                            s0_rq_valid := false;
                            s0_m_state  := m_idle;
                        } else { if s0_rq_valid {
                            if ~port_used {
                                ev_ans     := true;
                                ev_ans_h   := s0_m_base;
                                ev_ans_o   := s0_rq_off;
                                ev_ans_d   := hw_lsel(dat(v0), s0_rq_off);
                                ev_ans_idx := s0_rq_idx;
                                port_used  := true;
                                s0_rq_valid := false;
                                s0_m_state  := m_idle;
                            };
                        } else {
                            s0_m_state := m_idle;
                        } };
                    };
                }

                action step_1(ae0 : bool) = {
                    if s1_m_state = m_fill {
                        if tag_occ(v1) {
                            if ~ae0 {
                                ae_r := true;
                                ae_line := s1_mvline;
                                ae_dty  := tag_dty(v1);
                                ae_dat  := dat(v1);
                                tag_occ(v1) := false;
                            };
                        } else {
                            dat(v1)     := s1_mfb;
                            tag_ln(v1)  := s1_m_base;
                            tag_dty(v1) := false;
                            tag_exc(v1) := s1_m_excl;
                            tag_occ(v1) := true;
                            if s1_m_vway = 1 { set_lru(hw_hash(s1_m_base)) := 0; }
                            else { set_lru(hw_hash(s1_m_base)) := 1; };
                            s1_m_state := m_replay;
                        };
                    } else {
                        if s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen {
                            dat(v1) := hw_lins(dat(v1), s1_rq_off, s1_rq_wdata);
                            tag_dty(v1) := true;
                            tag_exc(v1) := true;
                            s1_rq_valid := false;
                            s1_m_state  := m_idle;
                        } else { if s1_rq_valid {
                            if ~port_used {
                                ev_ans     := true;
                                ev_ans_h   := s1_m_base;
                                ev_ans_o   := s1_rq_off;
                                ev_ans_d   := hw_lsel(dat(v1), s1_rq_off);
                                ev_ans_idx := s1_rq_idx;
                                port_used  := true;
                                s1_rq_valid := false;
                                s1_m_state  := m_idle;
                            };
                        } else {
                            s1_m_state := m_idle;
                        } };
                    };
                }

                after posedge {
                    var ae0 : bool := ae_r;
                    # ---- the PRE-edge control states.  Channel 1's handshake
                    # has to be resolved against the states the directory
                    # sampled, not against the ones an invalidate bounce
                    # produced on this same edge: a bounce manufactures a
                    # fresh `m_req`, and advancing THAT slot would hand it a
                    # grant the other slot asked for. ----
                    var q0 : mstate := s0_m_state;
                    var q1 : mstate := s1_m_state;

                    port_used := false;
                    ev_ans    := false;

                    # ---- THE array action, exactly one.  Invalidation
                    # outranks the stage and both MSHRs: a
                    # granted-but-unreplayed line is clean, so bouncing it
                    # keeps the parked request and simply re-requests. ----
                    if inv_act & ~ae_r {
                        ae_r := true;
                        ae_line := c0_inv_line;
                        ae_dty  := (tag_dty(inv_slot) if inv_hit else false);
                        ae_dat  := (dat(inv_slot) if inv_hit else 0);
                        if inv_hit {
                            tag_occ(inv_slot) := false;
                        };
                        if (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                                & s0_m_base = c0_inv_line {
                            s0_m_state := m_req;
                        };
                        if (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                                & s1_m_base = c0_inv_line {
                            s1_m_state := m_req;
                        };
                    } else { if p_valid & ~inv_act
                            & ~(s0_m_state ~= m_idle & s0_m_base = p_h)
                            & ~(s1_m_state ~= m_idle & s1_m_base = p_h)
                            & ~(s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                            & ~(s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                            & (s0_m_state = m_idle | s1_m_state = m_idle)
                            & (~p_wen | ~p_present | p_hexcl | ~ae_r) {
                        call serve_stage;
                    } else { if ~inv_act
                                      & (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay) {
                        call step_0(ae0);
                    } else { if ~inv_act
                                      & (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay) {
                        call step_1(ae0);
                    } } } };

                    # ---- channel 2: latch the matching Shared/Exclusive
                    # grant and reserve a victim way, away from whatever the
                    # other MSHR is already aiming at in the same set. ----
                    if s0_m_state = m_wait & c0_gr_valid & ~c0_inv_valid
                            & s0_m_base = c0_gr_line
                            & (~s0_rq_wen | c0_gr_excl) {
                        s0_mfb := c0_gr_data;
                        s0_m_excl := c0_gr_excl;
                        var w0 : way := 1;
                        if set_lru(hw_hash(s0_m_base)) = 1 { w0 := 0; };
                        if (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                                & hw_hash(s1_m_base) = hw_hash(s0_m_base) {
                            if s1_m_vway = 0 { w0 := 1; } else { w0 := 0; };
                        };
                        s0_m_vway  := w0;
                        s0_mvline  := tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(s0_m_base), w0));
                        s0_m_state := m_fill;
                    };
                    if s1_m_state = m_wait & c0_gr_valid & ~c0_inv_valid
                            & s1_m_base = c0_gr_line
                            & (~s1_rq_wen | c0_gr_excl) {
                        s1_mfb := c0_gr_data;
                        s1_m_excl := c0_gr_excl;
                        var w1 : way := 1;
                        if set_lru(hw_hash(s1_m_base)) = 1 { w1 := 0; };
                        if (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                                & hw_hash(s0_m_base) = hw_hash(s1_m_base) {
                            if s0_m_vway = 0 { w1 := 1; } else { w1 := 0; };
                        };
                        s1_m_vway  := w1;
                        s1_mvline  := tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(s1_m_base), w1));
                        s1_m_state := m_fill;
                    };

                    # ---- channel 1, resolved against the pre-edge states ----
                    if c0_req_taken {
                        if q0 = m_req {
                            s0_m_state := m_wait;
                        } else { if q1 = m_req {
                            s1_m_state := m_wait;
                        } };
                    };

                    # ---- stage intake, after the move ----
                    if lsq_to_cache_req_en0 & ~p_valid {
                        p_valid := true;
                        p_h := lsq_to_cache_req_h0;   p_o := lsq_to_cache_req_o0;
                        p_wen := lsq_to_cache_req_wen0;
                        p_wdata := lsq_to_cache_req_wdata0;
                        p_idx := lsq_to_cache_req_idx0;
                    };

                    # ---- channel 3 retires, off the PRE-edge slot ----
                    if ae0 & c0_ack_taken {
                        ae_r := false;
                    };
                }

                # ==========================================================
                # 3.1  Array data and permissions.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [c_set_p0] occupied(K, W) -> hash(ltag(K, W)) = K
                invariant [c_uniq_p0] occupied(K, W1) & occupied(K, W2)
                    & ltag(K, W1) = ltag(K, W2) -> W1 = W2
                invariant [c_img_p0] occupied(K, W) ->
                    val(K, W, O) = img(ltag(K, W), O)
                invariant [c_clean_p0] occupied(K, W) & ~dirty(K, W) ->
                    ~dirtied0(ltag(K, W), O)
                invariant [dirty_excl_p0] occupied(K, W) & dirty(K, W) ->
                    exclusive(K, W)
                invariant [hold_inf_a_p0] occupied(K, W) -> holds0(ltag(K, W))
                invariant [excl_inf_a_p0] occupied(K, W) & exclusive(K, W) ->
                    exclusive0(ltag(K, W))

                # ==========================================================
                # 3.2  The two MSHRs do not alias the array, or each other.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [no_alias_0_p0]
                    (s0_m_state = m_req | s0_m_state = m_wait | s0_m_state = m_fill)
                    & occupied(K, W) -> ltag(K, W) ~= s0_m_base
                invariant [no_alias_1_p0]
                    (s1_m_state = m_req | s1_m_state = m_wait | s1_m_state = m_fill)
                    & occupied(K, W) -> ltag(K, W) ~= s1_m_base
                invariant [mshr_distinct_p0] s0_m_state ~= m_idle & s1_m_state ~= m_idle
                    -> s0_m_base ~= s1_m_base

                # ---- a filling MSHR owns its victim way, and the two never
                # aim at the same one ----
                invariant [mv_ok_0_p0] s0_m_state = m_fill & tag_occ(v0) ->
                    s0_mvline = ltag(hash(s0_m_base), s0_m_vway)
                invariant [mv_ok_1_p0] s1_m_state = m_fill & tag_occ(v1) ->
                    s1_mvline = ltag(hash(s1_m_base), s1_m_vway)
                invariant [ev_distinct_p0]
                      (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                    & (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                    & hash(s0_m_base) = hash(s1_m_base)
                    -> s0_m_vway ~= s1_m_vway

                # ---- past the fill the line really is where the MSHR left
                # it, which is what the replay writes into ----
                invariant [replay_way_0_p0] s0_m_state = m_replay ->
                    tag_occ(v0) & ltag(hash(s0_m_base), s0_m_vway) = s0_m_base
                invariant [replay_way_1_p0] s1_m_state = m_replay ->
                    tag_occ(v1) & ltag(hash(s1_m_base), s1_m_vway) = s1_m_base

                # ---- a parked store only ever sits behind an Exclusive
                # request: that is what makes the replay's write legal ----
                invariant [rq_store_excl_0_p0] s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen -> s0_m_excl
                invariant [rq_store_excl_1_p0] s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen -> s1_m_excl

                # ==========================================================
                # 3.3  The fill buffers.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [fill_clean_0_p0] s0_m_state = m_fill ->
                    ~dirtied0(s0_m_base, O)
                invariant [fill_clean_1_p0] s1_m_state = m_fill ->
                    ~dirtied0(s1_m_base, O)
                invariant [mfb_img_0_p0] s0_m_state = m_fill ->
                    lsel(s0_mfb, O) = img(s0_m_base, O)
                invariant [mfb_img_1_p0] s1_m_state = m_fill ->
                    lsel(s1_mfb, O) = img(s1_m_base, O)

                invariant [hold_inf_0_p0]
                    (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay) ->
                    holds0(s0_m_base)
                invariant [hold_inf_1_p0]
                    (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay) ->
                    holds0(s1_m_base)
                invariant [excl_inf_0_p0]
                    (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay) & s0_m_excl ->
                    exclusive0(s0_m_base)
                invariant [excl_inf_1_p0]
                    (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay) & s1_m_excl ->
                    exclusive0(s1_m_base)

                # ---- the concrete channel-3 register mirrors cachemem. ----
                invariant [ae_sync_p0] ack0_g_en = ae_r

            }

        } with this, dir_body, addr_thy, basis, data_impl
        # ==================================================================
        # CPU 1's cache.  8 sets x 2 ways, 2 MSHRs, whole-line data.
        #
        # The beat machinery is gone from this side entirely: a line is
        # fetched in ONE grant and written back in ONE ack, so the two-beat
        # ladder that used to live in the MSHR now lives once, in the
        # directory's memory-facing sequencer.  What is left of an MSHR is
        # `m_req -> m_wait -> m_fill -> m_replay`: hold channel 1, latch the
        # grant into a whole-line fill buffer, clear a way for it, then serve
        # the request that missed against the array.
        #
        # ONE array action per edge.  The arbiter takes the staged request if
        # its gate is open and otherwise steps the first MSHR that wants the
        # array, and that is what lets a single `c_st_*` net carry both
        # commit shapes -- the store-hit drain and the replay store-apply --
        # without them ever racing.
        #
        # EVERY eviction is non-silent (plan decision 1): clean or dirty,
        # the victim goes out on channel 3, so the directory's sharer bit is
        # exact and it never has to recall a line it has lost track of.  A
        # give-up therefore cannot start until the channel-3 slot is free.
        # ==================================================================
        isolate cache1_body = {

            # ---- LSQ request channel: the request comes from the LSQ, the
            # ready is driven here.  `lsq_to_cache_req_idx1` is opaque to the
            # cache: it is stored beside the request in the MSHR slot and
            # handed straight back on the response. ----
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_en1     : bool
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_h1      : line
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_o1      : woffset
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_wen1    : bool
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_wdata1  : word
            wire lsq_to_cache_req_idx1    : load_idx
            wire cache_to_lsq_creq_ready1 : bool

            # ---- LSQ response channel: all driven here ----
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1 : bool
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_h1     : line
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_o1     : woffset
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_data1  : word
            wire cache_to_lsq_resp_idx1   : load_idx

            # ---- the ports this cache drives on the directory side ----
            wire req_en    : bool
            wire req_line  : line
            wire req_excl  : bool
            wire gr_taken  : bool
            wire inv_taken : bool

            # ---- channel 3 as REAL hardware, held by its producer (plan
            # decision 2).  `cachemem` keeps a ghost copy of the same slot --
            # the directory's `[wb_*]` invariants are claims about the
            # POST-state and need a symbol the environment cannot re-drive --
            # and `[ack_sync_*]` pins the two together, the `ae_r` pattern
            # widened from the enable bit to the whole payload.  Without these
            # registers the directory's datapath reads specification state and
            # `ivy_to_rtl` emits a dangling wire. ----
            wire ack_en    : bool
            wire ack_line  : line
            wire ack_dty   : bool
            wire ack_dat   : cache_line

            # ---- the pending-store view the LSQ contract is stated over.
            # A store is accepted at the port one edge before it reaches the
            # array: it waits in the stage, and on a miss it waits again in
            # the allocating MSHR's slot.  Those three records ARE
            # `in_progress`, and they are exported as wires so the contract
            # can name them without naming the registers behind them. ----
            wire stage_go : bool
            wire ps_v : bool
            wire ps_h : line
            wire ps_o : woffset
            wire ps_d : word
            wire p0_v : bool
            wire p0_h : line
            wire p0_o : woffset
            wire p0_d : word
            wire p1_v : bool
            wire p1_h : line
            wire p1_o : woffset
            wire p1_d : word
            wire in_progress(H : line) : bool

            # ---- proof observation ports ----
            wire st_en     : bool
            wire st_h      : line
            wire st_o      : woffset
            wire st_d      : word
            wire give_en   : bool
            wire give_line : line
            wire give_dty  : bool
            wire give_dat  : cache_line

            # ==============================================================
            # The contract.
            #
            # `[resp_img]` is the LSQ-facing half: the datum handed back is
            # the image at the address the response echoes.  Responses always
            # come out of the array and `[c_img_p1]` pins the array to `img`, so
            # it holds unconditionally -- phase 4 restates it weakly, over
            # `in_progress`, without the implementation changing.
            #
            # The rest is the directory-facing half, and it is the same seven
            # facts the phase-2 stand-in exported, generalised from one held
            # line to a set: a line under request is not held and has no ack
            # outstanding; a store only ever lands in a held line; a give-up
            # does not collide with a live ack or with a store; and -- the
            # cache's half of the old `[unowned]` -- a dirtied word lives in
            # a held line or in the dirty ack carrying it.
            # ==============================================================
            specification {
                # TODO: why does this stronger invariant hold?
                invariant [resp_img_int_p1] cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1 ->
                    cache_to_lsq_resp_data1 = img(cache_to_lsq_resp_h1,
                                                 cache_to_lsq_resp_o1)

                # the data we give to the LSQ is valid. 
                invariant [resp_quiet_p1] cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1
                    & ~in_progress(cache_to_lsq_resp_h1) ->
                    cache_to_lsq_resp_data1 = img(cache_to_lsq_resp_h1,
                                                 cache_to_lsq_resp_o1)

                invariant [resp_inprog_p1] cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1
                    & in_progress(cache_to_lsq_resp_h1) ->
                      cache_to_lsq_resp_data1 = img(cache_to_lsq_resp_h1,
                                                   cache_to_lsq_resp_o1)
                    | (ps_v & ps_h = cache_to_lsq_resp_h1
                       & ps_o = cache_to_lsq_resp_o1
                       & cache_to_lsq_resp_data1 = ps_d)
                    | (p0_v & p0_h = cache_to_lsq_resp_h1
                       & p0_o = cache_to_lsq_resp_o1
                       & cache_to_lsq_resp_data1 = p0_d)
                    | (p1_v & p1_h = cache_to_lsq_resp_h1
                       & p1_o = cache_to_lsq_resp_o1
                       & cache_to_lsq_resp_data1 = p1_d)

                
                # The cache only surrenders lines it holds
                invariant [ca_gv_hold_p1] c1_give_en -> holds1(c1_give_line)
                # Guarantee to the directory that if we don't hold, then
                # not exclusive
                invariant [ca_excl_hold_p1] exclusive1(H) -> holds1(H)

                # Storing a line implies that we have exclusive perms
                invariant [ca_st_excl_p1] c1_st_en -> exclusive1(c1_st_h)

                # Cache only takes the invalidate line if it has a line to invalidate
                invariant [ca_inv_reply_p1] c1_inv_taken ->
                    c1_give_en & c1_give_line = c1_inv_line

                invariant [ca_gv_nodouble_p1] c1_give_en -> ~ack1_g_en

                invariant [ca_gv_nost_p1] c1_give_en -> ~c1_st_en

                # A clean surrender carries no write debt: no word of the
                # line is dirtied, so the directory may keep trusting its
                # own copy of it.
                invariant [ca_gv_clean_p1] c1_give_en & ~c1_give_dty ->
                    ~dirtied1(c1_give_line, O)
                # A dirty surrender is believable as the whole image only
                # under SWMR: dirty implies the copy was Exclusive.
                invariant [ca_gv_dirty_excl_p1] c1_give_en & c1_give_dty ->
                    exclusive1(c1_give_line)

                # Write debt never floats unaccounted: a word is dirtied
                # only while its line is held or while the dirty ack
                # carrying it is in flight.
                invariant [ca_dirtied_p1]
                      ~holds1(H)
                    & ~(ack1_g_en & ack1_g_dty & ack1_g_line = H)
                    -> ~dirtied1(H, O)
                invariant [ca_dirtied_excl_p1] dirtied1(H, O) ->
                      exclusive1(H)
                    | (ack1_g_en & ack1_g_dty & ack1_g_line = H)

                # A clean ack vouches for nothing: no word of its line is dirtied.
                invariant [ca_clean_ack_p1] ack1_g_en & ~ack1_g_dty ->
                    ~dirtied1(ack1_g_line, O)

                # If we send an invalidate acknowledgement, then we don't hold the line
                invariant [ca_ack_idle_p1] ack1_g_en -> ~holds1(ack1_g_line)

                # Data we send as part of the invalidate is valid
                # TODO: how does this work w/ consistency model?
                invariant [ack_img_p1] ack1_g_en & ack1_g_dty ->
                    lsel(ack1_g_data, O) = img(ack1_g_line, O)

                # ---- the hardware slot and the ghost slot are the same slot ----
                invariant [ack_sync_en_p1] c1_ack_en = ack1_g_en
                invariant [ack_sync_line_p1] ack1_g_en ->
                    c1_ack_line = ack1_g_line
                invariant [ack_sync_dty_p1] ack1_g_en ->
                    c1_ack_dty = ack1_g_dty
                invariant [ack_sync_dat_p1] ack1_g_en ->
                    c1_ack_dat = ack1_g_data
            }

            implementation {
                # ==========================================================
                # The array.  One memory per former struct field, indexed by
                # the packed (set, way) slot -- an RTLIL memory is
                # one-dimensional and a struct sort has no bit width.  The
                # data is ONE whole line per slot now: a fill writes it in a
                # single edge, so there is nothing left for the beat split to
                # buy.
                #
                # `set_lru` is ONE bit per set, not one per way: per way it
                # took two writes to the same memory on every hit, which the
                # translator merges into a single port.
                # ==========================================================
                # `tag_exc` is written at a fill, off the grant's kind, and
                # RE-ASSERTED on the two store paths.  The re-assert is
                # provably idempotent -- a store only commits where the
                # permission already is -- but stating it syntactically is
                # what keeps `[ca_gv_clean_*]` inductive: without it the
                # solver has to rediscover the permission through the hit
                # test, and the query stops coming back.
                var tag_occ(S : cache_slot) : bool
                var tag_dty(S : cache_slot) : bool
                var tag_exc(S : cache_slot) : bool
                var tag_ln(S : cache_slot)  : line
                var set_lru(K : key)        : bit
                var dat(S : cache_slot)     : cache_line

                # ---- the (set, way) view the invariants are written in.
                # Plain defined functions, so `ivy_to_rtl` never sees them. ----
                function occupied(K : key, W : way) : bool
                definition occupied(K, W) = tag_occ(cidx(K, W))
                function dirty(K : key, W : way) : bool
                definition dirty(K, W) = tag_dty(cidx(K, W))
                function exclusive(K : key, W : way) : bool
                definition exclusive(K, W) = tag_exc(cidx(K, W))
                function ltag(K : key, W : way) : line
                definition ltag(K, W) = tag_ln(cidx(K, W))
                function val(K : key, W : way, O : woffset) : word
                definition val(K, W, O) = lsel(dat(cidx(K, W)), O)

                # ---- the MSHR's control states.  An enumeration has no bit
                # width, so these are narrow bit vectors and the names are
                # 0-ary wires. ----
                wire m_idle   : mstate
                wire m_req    : mstate
                wire m_wait   : mstate
                wire m_fill   : mstate
                wire m_replay : mstate
                definition m_idle   = 0
                definition m_req    = 1
                definition m_wait   = 2
                definition m_fill   = 3
                definition m_replay = 4

                # ==========================================================
                # The two MSHRs, as registers rather than as an array over
                # `mshr_id`: `m_state` is written by the capture, by an
                # allocation and by a fill on the same edge, and a memory has
                # one write port per clock.  The proof sees them only at the
                # two literal indices, which is what keeps every invariant a
                # clean trigger.
                #
                # `mvline` is GHOST: the line this MSHR is evicting, recorded
                # when the victim way is picked.  Metadata carried BESIDE the
                # slot rather than read back out of it, which is what keeps
                # the writeback proof inside the decidable fragment -- naming
                # the victim as `ltag(hash(m_base(Id)), ...)` under a
                # quantifier sends the query from a line, through `hash`,
                # into the array and back out to a line.
                # ==========================================================
                var s0_m_state : mstate
                var s0_m_base  : line
                var s0_m_excl  : bool
                var s0_m_vway  : way
                var s0_mvline  : line
                var s0_mfb     : cache_line
                var s0_rq_valid : bool
                var s0_rq_off   : woffset
                var s0_rq_wen   : bool
                var s0_rq_wdata : word
                var s0_rq_idx   : load_idx
                var s1_m_state : mstate
                var s1_m_base  : line
                var s1_m_excl  : bool
                var s1_m_vway  : way
                var s1_mvline  : line
                var s1_mfb     : cache_line
                var s1_rq_valid : bool
                var s1_rq_off   : woffset
                var s1_rq_wen   : bool
                var s1_rq_wdata : word
                var s1_rq_idx   : load_idx

                # ---- the input stage.  ALL requests pass through it. ----
                var p_valid : bool
                var p_h     : line
                var p_o     : woffset
                var p_wen   : bool
                var p_wdata : word
                var p_idx   : load_idx

                var port_used : bool

                var ev_ans     : bool
                var ev_ans_h   : line
                var ev_ans_o   : woffset
                var ev_ans_d   : word
                var ev_ans_idx : load_idx

                # ---- this side's copy of the channel-3 slot.  A guard has
                # to be a function of THIS isolate's registers: the record at
                # `cachemem` is written by a monitor that runs before this
                # one, so a guard phrased over `ack1_g_en` would be read there
                # at its old value and re-read here at its new one. ----
                var ae_r : bool
                var ae_line : line
                var ae_dty  : bool
                var ae_dat  : cache_line

                after init {
                    tag_occ(S) := false; tag_dty(S) := false; tag_exc(S) := false; tag_ln(S) := 0;
                    set_lru(K) := 0; dat(S) := 0;
                    s0_m_state := 0; s0_m_base := 0; s0_m_excl := false;
                    s0_m_vway := 0; s0_mvline := 0; s0_mfb := 0;
                    s0_rq_valid := false; s0_rq_off := 0; s0_rq_wen := false;
                    s0_rq_wdata := 0; s0_rq_idx := 0;
                    s1_m_state := 0; s1_m_base := 0; s1_m_excl := false;
                    s1_m_vway := 0; s1_mvline := 0; s1_mfb := 0;
                    s1_rq_valid := false; s1_rq_off := 0; s1_rq_wen := false;
                    s1_rq_wdata := 0; s1_rq_idx := 0;
                    p_valid := false; p_h := 0; p_o := 0; p_wen := false;
                    p_wdata := 0; p_idx := 0;
                    port_used := false;
                    ev_ans := false; ev_ans_h := 0; ev_ans_o := 0;
                    ev_ans_d := 0; ev_ans_idx := 0;
                    ae_r := false;
                    ae_line := 0; ae_dty := false; ae_dat := 0;
                }

                # ==========================================================
                # The arbiter, written out as three wires.  `stage_go` is the
                # stage-movable gate: the staged request enters only when its
                # line aliases no active MSHR, no MSHR wants the array this
                # edge, and an MSHR is free to take a miss.  Otherwise the
                # first MSHR that wants the array gets it.  Exactly one of
                # the three is true, which is what makes the single-writer
                # discipline on the array and on `c_st_*` syntactic.
                #
                # The gate does NOT mention the response port.  It does not
                # have to: one array action per edge means a store commit and
                # a load answer never share an edge, so `[resp_img]` cannot
                # be overtaken by an image move.
                # ==========================================================
                # ---- presence and permission are separate.  Loads hit any
                # resident copy; stores hit only an Exclusive copy. ----
                wire p_present : bool
                wire p_hit     : bool
                wire p_hway    : way
                wire p_hexcl   : bool
                wire p_slot    : cache_slot
                definition p_present =
                      (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 1)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 1)) = p_h)
                    | (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 0)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 0)) = p_h)
                definition p_hway =
                    (1 if (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 1))
                           & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), 1)) = p_h) else 0)
                definition p_slot = hw_cidx(hw_hash(p_h), p_hway)
                definition p_hexcl = p_present & tag_exc(p_slot)
                definition p_hit = p_present & (~p_wen | p_hexcl)
                wire arb0 : bool
                wire arb1 : bool

                # ---- invalidates use a separate held slot and have array and
                # channel-3 priority.  An invalidate is answered for a
                # resident copy AND for either MSHR's post-grant window,
                # where the line is held but not yet installed: that MSHR
                # bounces back to `m_req` with its request still parked, and
                # the reply is clean because the parked store applies only at
                # replay.  At most one of the three can match: `[c_uniq]`
                # keeps a line in one way and `[mshr_distinct_p1]` keeps the
                # two MSHRs off each other's line. ----
                wire inv_hit    : bool
                wire inv_hway   : way
                wire inv_slot   : cache_slot
                wire inv_mshr0  : bool
                wire inv_mshr1  : bool
                wire inv_act    : bool
                definition inv_hit =
                      (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c1_inv_line), 1)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c1_inv_line), 1)) = c1_inv_line)
                    | (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c1_inv_line), 0)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c1_inv_line), 0)) = c1_inv_line)
                definition inv_hway =
                    (1 if (tag_occ(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c1_inv_line), 1))
                           & tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(c1_inv_line), 1)) = c1_inv_line) else 0)
                definition inv_slot = hw_cidx(hw_hash(c1_inv_line), inv_hway)
                definition inv_mshr0 = (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                    & s0_m_base = c1_inv_line
                definition inv_mshr1 = (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                    & s1_m_base = c1_inv_line
                definition inv_act = c1_inv_valid & (inv_hit | inv_mshr0 | inv_mshr1)
                definition inv_taken = inv_act & ~ae_r

                # ---- the stage-movable gate, back to its two-MSHR shape:
                # the staged request enters only when its line aliases no
                # active MSHR, no MSHR wants the array this edge, and an MSHR
                # is free to take a miss -- plus, new here, channel 3 is free
                # if the request is a store that has to self-downgrade a
                # Shared copy first.  Otherwise the first MSHR that wants the
                # array gets it, and an invalidate outranks all of them. ----
                definition stage_go = p_valid
                    & ~inv_act
                    & ~(s0_m_state ~= m_idle & s0_m_base = p_h)
                    & ~(s1_m_state ~= m_idle & s1_m_base = p_h)
                    & ~(s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                    & ~(s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                    & (s0_m_state = m_idle | s1_m_state = m_idle)
                    & (~p_wen | ~p_present | p_hexcl | ~ae_r)
                definition arb0 = ~stage_go & ~inv_act
                    & (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                definition arb1 = ~stage_go & ~inv_act
                    & ~(s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                    & (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)

                definition ps_v = p_valid & p_wen
                definition ps_h = p_h
                definition ps_o = p_o
                definition ps_d = p_wdata
                definition p0_v = s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen
                definition p0_h = s0_m_base
                definition p0_o = s0_rq_off
                definition p0_d = s0_rq_wdata
                definition p1_v = s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen
                definition p1_h = s1_m_base
                definition p1_o = s1_rq_off
                definition p1_d = s1_rq_wdata
                definition in_progress(H) =
                      (p_valid & p_wen & p_h = H)
                    | (s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen & s0_m_base = H)
                    | (s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen & s1_m_base = H)

                # ---- the victim slot each MSHR is aiming at ----
                wire v0 : cache_slot
                wire v1 : cache_slot
                definition v0 = hw_cidx(hw_hash(s0_m_base), s0_m_vway)
                definition v1 = hw_cidx(hw_hash(s1_m_base), s1_m_vway)

                # ---- channel 3 has four producers now: the invalidate
                # reply, the store's self-downgrade, and each MSHR's
                # fill-eviction.  They are one-hot because the invalidate
                # outranks everything and `stage_go`, `arb0`, `arb1` are
                # mutually exclusive. ----
                wire gv0 : bool
                wire gv1 : bool
                wire ugv : bool
                definition gv0 = (s0_m_state = m_fill) & tag_occ(v0) & ~ae_r
                definition gv1 = (s1_m_state = m_fill) & tag_occ(v1) & ~ae_r
                definition ugv = stage_go & p_wen & p_present & ~p_hexcl

                definition give_en = inv_taken | ugv | (arb0 & gv0) | (arb1 & gv1)
                definition give_line = (c1_inv_line if inv_taken else
                                        (p_h if ugv else
                                         (s0_mvline if arb0 else s1_mvline)))
                definition give_dty = ((tag_dty(inv_slot) if inv_hit else false) if inv_taken else
                                       (tag_dty(p_slot) if ugv else
                                        (tag_dty(v0) if arb0 else tag_dty(v1))))
                definition give_dat = ((dat(inv_slot) if inv_hit else 0) if inv_taken else
                                       (dat(p_slot) if ugv else
                                        (dat(v0) if arb0 else dat(v1))))

                # ---- a store commits on the stage's Exclusive-hit path or on
                # an MSHR's replay ----
                definition st_en = (stage_go & p_wen & p_hit)
                    | (arb0 & s0_m_state = m_replay & s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen)
                    | (arb1 & s1_m_state = m_replay & s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen)
                definition st_h = (p_h if stage_go
                                   else (s0_m_base if arb0 else s1_m_base))
                definition st_o = (p_o if stage_go
                                   else (s0_rq_off if arb0 else s1_rq_off))
                definition st_d = (p_wdata if stage_go
                                   else (s0_rq_wdata if arb0 else s1_rq_wdata))

                # ---- channel 1 is held by one `m_req` MSHR at a time, MSHR0
                # first ----
                definition req_en   = (s0_m_state = m_req) | (s1_m_state = m_req)
                definition req_line = (s0_m_base if s0_m_state = m_req
                                       else s1_m_base)
                definition req_excl = (s0_m_excl if s0_m_state = m_req
                                       else s1_m_excl)

                # ---- a grant is consumed the edge it matches a waiting
                # MSHR, and only if its kind covers what that MSHR parked:
                # `gr_line = m_base` is unambiguous because the two MSHRs
                # never fetch the same line (`[mshr_distinct_p1]`) ----
                definition gr_taken = c1_gr_valid & ~c1_inv_valid
                    & ((s0_m_state = m_wait & s0_m_base = c1_gr_line
                        & (~s0_rq_wen | c1_gr_excl))
                       | (s1_m_state = m_wait & s1_m_base = c1_gr_line
                          & (~s1_rq_wen | c1_gr_excl)))

                definition ack_en   = ae_r
                definition ack_line = ae_line
                definition ack_dty  = ae_dty
                definition ack_dat  = ae_dat

                definition cache_to_lsq_creq_ready1 = ~p_valid | stage_go

                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1 = ev_ans
                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_h1     = ev_ans_h
                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_o1     = ev_ans_o
                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_data1  = ev_ans_d
                definition cache_to_lsq_resp_idx1   = ev_ans_idx

                # ==========================================================
                # The three array actions.
                # ==========================================================
                action serve_stage = {
                    var h  : line    := p_h;
                    var o  : woffset := p_o;
                    var rs : key     := hw_hash(h);
                    var present : bool := false;
                    var hit     : bool := false;
                    var hway    : way  := 1;
                    p_valid := false;
                    if tag_occ(hw_cidx(rs, 1)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(rs, 1)) = h {
                        present := true; hway := 1;
                    } else { if tag_occ(hw_cidx(rs, 0)) & tag_ln(hw_cidx(rs, 0)) = h {
                        present := true; hway := 0;
                    } };
                    hit := present & (~p_wen | tag_exc(hw_cidx(rs, hway)));
                    if hit {
                        if p_wen {
                            dat(hw_cidx(rs, hway)) :=
                                hw_lins(dat(hw_cidx(rs, hway)), o, p_wdata);
                            tag_dty(hw_cidx(rs, hway)) := true;
                            tag_exc(hw_cidx(rs, hway)) := true;
                        } else {
                            ev_ans     := true;
                            ev_ans_h   := h;
                            ev_ans_o   := o;
                            ev_ans_d   := hw_lsel(dat(hw_cidx(rs, hway)), o);
                            ev_ans_idx := p_idx;
                            port_used  := true;
                        };
                        if hway = 1 { set_lru(rs) := 0; } else { set_lru(rs) := 1; };
                    } else {
                        # A store to Shared surrenders that clean copy before
                        # requesting Exclusive.  `stage_go` waited for ch3.
                        if present {
                            ae_r := true;
                            ae_line := h;
                            ae_dty  := tag_dty(hw_cidx(rs, hway));
                            ae_dat  := dat(hw_cidx(rs, hway));
                            tag_occ(hw_cidx(rs, hway)) := false;
                        };
                        # ---- allocate.  Under the gate one MSHR is idle, so
                        # the else arm is closed by construction. ----
                        if s0_m_state = m_idle {
                            s0_m_state := m_req;
                            s0_m_base  := h;
                            s0_m_excl  := p_wen;
                            s0_rq_valid := true; s0_rq_off := o;
                            s0_rq_wen := p_wen; s0_rq_wdata := p_wdata;
                            s0_rq_idx := p_idx;
                        } else {
                            s1_m_state := m_req;
                            s1_m_base  := h;
                            s1_m_excl  := p_wen;
                            s1_rq_valid := true; s1_rq_off := o;
                            s1_rq_wen := p_wen; s1_rq_wdata := p_wdata;
                            s1_rq_idx := p_idx;
                        };
                    };
                }

                action step_0(ae0 : bool) = {
                    if s0_m_state = m_fill {
                        if tag_occ(v0) {
                            if ~ae0 {
                                ae_r := true;
                                ae_line := s0_mvline;
                                ae_dty  := tag_dty(v0);
                                ae_dat  := dat(v0);
                                tag_occ(v0) := false;
                            };
                        } else {
                            dat(v0)     := s0_mfb;
                            tag_ln(v0)  := s0_m_base;
                            tag_dty(v0) := false;
                            tag_exc(v0) := s0_m_excl;
                            tag_occ(v0) := true;
                            if s0_m_vway = 1 { set_lru(hw_hash(s0_m_base)) := 0; }
                            else { set_lru(hw_hash(s0_m_base)) := 1; };
                            s0_m_state := m_replay;
                        };
                    } else {
                        if s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen {
                            dat(v0) := hw_lins(dat(v0), s0_rq_off, s0_rq_wdata);
                            tag_dty(v0) := true;
                            tag_exc(v0) := true;
                            s0_rq_valid := false;
                            s0_m_state  := m_idle;
                        } else { if s0_rq_valid {
                            if ~port_used {
                                ev_ans     := true;
                                ev_ans_h   := s0_m_base;
                                ev_ans_o   := s0_rq_off;
                                ev_ans_d   := hw_lsel(dat(v0), s0_rq_off);
                                ev_ans_idx := s0_rq_idx;
                                port_used  := true;
                                s0_rq_valid := false;
                                s0_m_state  := m_idle;
                            };
                        } else {
                            s0_m_state := m_idle;
                        } };
                    };
                }

                action step_1(ae0 : bool) = {
                    if s1_m_state = m_fill {
                        if tag_occ(v1) {
                            if ~ae0 {
                                ae_r := true;
                                ae_line := s1_mvline;
                                ae_dty  := tag_dty(v1);
                                ae_dat  := dat(v1);
                                tag_occ(v1) := false;
                            };
                        } else {
                            dat(v1)     := s1_mfb;
                            tag_ln(v1)  := s1_m_base;
                            tag_dty(v1) := false;
                            tag_exc(v1) := s1_m_excl;
                            tag_occ(v1) := true;
                            if s1_m_vway = 1 { set_lru(hw_hash(s1_m_base)) := 0; }
                            else { set_lru(hw_hash(s1_m_base)) := 1; };
                            s1_m_state := m_replay;
                        };
                    } else {
                        if s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen {
                            dat(v1) := hw_lins(dat(v1), s1_rq_off, s1_rq_wdata);
                            tag_dty(v1) := true;
                            tag_exc(v1) := true;
                            s1_rq_valid := false;
                            s1_m_state  := m_idle;
                        } else { if s1_rq_valid {
                            if ~port_used {
                                ev_ans     := true;
                                ev_ans_h   := s1_m_base;
                                ev_ans_o   := s1_rq_off;
                                ev_ans_d   := hw_lsel(dat(v1), s1_rq_off);
                                ev_ans_idx := s1_rq_idx;
                                port_used  := true;
                                s1_rq_valid := false;
                                s1_m_state  := m_idle;
                            };
                        } else {
                            s1_m_state := m_idle;
                        } };
                    };
                }

                after posedge {
                    var ae0 : bool := ae_r;
                    # ---- the PRE-edge control states.  Channel 1's handshake
                    # has to be resolved against the states the directory
                    # sampled, not against the ones an invalidate bounce
                    # produced on this same edge: a bounce manufactures a
                    # fresh `m_req`, and advancing THAT slot would hand it a
                    # grant the other slot asked for. ----
                    var q0 : mstate := s0_m_state;
                    var q1 : mstate := s1_m_state;

                    port_used := false;
                    ev_ans    := false;

                    # ---- THE array action, exactly one.  Invalidation
                    # outranks the stage and both MSHRs: a
                    # granted-but-unreplayed line is clean, so bouncing it
                    # keeps the parked request and simply re-requests. ----
                    if inv_act & ~ae_r {
                        ae_r := true;
                        ae_line := c1_inv_line;
                        ae_dty  := (tag_dty(inv_slot) if inv_hit else false);
                        ae_dat  := (dat(inv_slot) if inv_hit else 0);
                        if inv_hit {
                            tag_occ(inv_slot) := false;
                        };
                        if (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                                & s0_m_base = c1_inv_line {
                            s0_m_state := m_req;
                        };
                        if (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                                & s1_m_base = c1_inv_line {
                            s1_m_state := m_req;
                        };
                    } else { if p_valid & ~inv_act
                            & ~(s0_m_state ~= m_idle & s0_m_base = p_h)
                            & ~(s1_m_state ~= m_idle & s1_m_base = p_h)
                            & ~(s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                            & ~(s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                            & (s0_m_state = m_idle | s1_m_state = m_idle)
                            & (~p_wen | ~p_present | p_hexcl | ~ae_r) {
                        call serve_stage;
                    } else { if ~inv_act
                                      & (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay) {
                        call step_0(ae0);
                    } else { if ~inv_act
                                      & (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay) {
                        call step_1(ae0);
                    } } } };

                    # ---- channel 2: latch the matching Shared/Exclusive
                    # grant and reserve a victim way, away from whatever the
                    # other MSHR is already aiming at in the same set. ----
                    if s0_m_state = m_wait & c1_gr_valid & ~c1_inv_valid
                            & s0_m_base = c1_gr_line
                            & (~s0_rq_wen | c1_gr_excl) {
                        s0_mfb := c1_gr_data;
                        s0_m_excl := c1_gr_excl;
                        var w0 : way := 1;
                        if set_lru(hw_hash(s0_m_base)) = 1 { w0 := 0; };
                        if (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                                & hw_hash(s1_m_base) = hw_hash(s0_m_base) {
                            if s1_m_vway = 0 { w0 := 1; } else { w0 := 0; };
                        };
                        s0_m_vway  := w0;
                        s0_mvline  := tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(s0_m_base), w0));
                        s0_m_state := m_fill;
                    };
                    if s1_m_state = m_wait & c1_gr_valid & ~c1_inv_valid
                            & s1_m_base = c1_gr_line
                            & (~s1_rq_wen | c1_gr_excl) {
                        s1_mfb := c1_gr_data;
                        s1_m_excl := c1_gr_excl;
                        var w1 : way := 1;
                        if set_lru(hw_hash(s1_m_base)) = 1 { w1 := 0; };
                        if (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                                & hw_hash(s0_m_base) = hw_hash(s1_m_base) {
                            if s0_m_vway = 0 { w1 := 1; } else { w1 := 0; };
                        };
                        s1_m_vway  := w1;
                        s1_mvline  := tag_ln(hw_cidx(hw_hash(s1_m_base), w1));
                        s1_m_state := m_fill;
                    };

                    # ---- channel 1, resolved against the pre-edge states ----
                    if c1_req_taken {
                        if q0 = m_req {
                            s0_m_state := m_wait;
                        } else { if q1 = m_req {
                            s1_m_state := m_wait;
                        } };
                    };

                    # ---- stage intake, after the move ----
                    if lsq_to_cache_req_en1 & ~p_valid {
                        p_valid := true;
                        p_h := lsq_to_cache_req_h1;   p_o := lsq_to_cache_req_o1;
                        p_wen := lsq_to_cache_req_wen1;
                        p_wdata := lsq_to_cache_req_wdata1;
                        p_idx := lsq_to_cache_req_idx1;
                    };

                    # ---- channel 3 retires, off the PRE-edge slot ----
                    if ae0 & c1_ack_taken {
                        ae_r := false;
                    };
                }

                # ==========================================================
                # 3.1  Array data and permissions.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [c_set_p1] occupied(K, W) -> hash(ltag(K, W)) = K
                invariant [c_uniq_p1] occupied(K, W1) & occupied(K, W2)
                    & ltag(K, W1) = ltag(K, W2) -> W1 = W2
                invariant [c_img_p1] occupied(K, W) ->
                    val(K, W, O) = img(ltag(K, W), O)
                invariant [c_clean_p1] occupied(K, W) & ~dirty(K, W) ->
                    ~dirtied1(ltag(K, W), O)
                invariant [dirty_excl_p1] occupied(K, W) & dirty(K, W) ->
                    exclusive(K, W)
                invariant [hold_inf_a_p1] occupied(K, W) -> holds1(ltag(K, W))
                invariant [excl_inf_a_p1] occupied(K, W) & exclusive(K, W) ->
                    exclusive1(ltag(K, W))

                # ==========================================================
                # 3.2  The two MSHRs do not alias the array, or each other.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [no_alias_0_p1]
                    (s0_m_state = m_req | s0_m_state = m_wait | s0_m_state = m_fill)
                    & occupied(K, W) -> ltag(K, W) ~= s0_m_base
                invariant [no_alias_1_p1]
                    (s1_m_state = m_req | s1_m_state = m_wait | s1_m_state = m_fill)
                    & occupied(K, W) -> ltag(K, W) ~= s1_m_base
                invariant [mshr_distinct_p1] s0_m_state ~= m_idle & s1_m_state ~= m_idle
                    -> s0_m_base ~= s1_m_base

                # ---- a filling MSHR owns its victim way, and the two never
                # aim at the same one ----
                invariant [mv_ok_0_p1] s0_m_state = m_fill & tag_occ(v0) ->
                    s0_mvline = ltag(hash(s0_m_base), s0_m_vway)
                invariant [mv_ok_1_p1] s1_m_state = m_fill & tag_occ(v1) ->
                    s1_mvline = ltag(hash(s1_m_base), s1_m_vway)
                invariant [ev_distinct_p1]
                      (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay)
                    & (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay)
                    & hash(s0_m_base) = hash(s1_m_base)
                    -> s0_m_vway ~= s1_m_vway

                # ---- past the fill the line really is where the MSHR left
                # it, which is what the replay writes into ----
                invariant [replay_way_0_p1] s0_m_state = m_replay ->
                    tag_occ(v0) & ltag(hash(s0_m_base), s0_m_vway) = s0_m_base
                invariant [replay_way_1_p1] s1_m_state = m_replay ->
                    tag_occ(v1) & ltag(hash(s1_m_base), s1_m_vway) = s1_m_base

                # ---- a parked store only ever sits behind an Exclusive
                # request: that is what makes the replay's write legal ----
                invariant [rq_store_excl_0_p1] s0_rq_valid & s0_rq_wen -> s0_m_excl
                invariant [rq_store_excl_1_p1] s1_rq_valid & s1_rq_wen -> s1_m_excl

                # ==========================================================
                # 3.3  The fill buffers.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [fill_clean_0_p1] s0_m_state = m_fill ->
                    ~dirtied1(s0_m_base, O)
                invariant [fill_clean_1_p1] s1_m_state = m_fill ->
                    ~dirtied1(s1_m_base, O)
                invariant [mfb_img_0_p1] s0_m_state = m_fill ->
                    lsel(s0_mfb, O) = img(s0_m_base, O)
                invariant [mfb_img_1_p1] s1_m_state = m_fill ->
                    lsel(s1_mfb, O) = img(s1_m_base, O)

                invariant [hold_inf_0_p1]
                    (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay) ->
                    holds1(s0_m_base)
                invariant [hold_inf_1_p1]
                    (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay) ->
                    holds1(s1_m_base)
                invariant [excl_inf_0_p1]
                    (s0_m_state = m_fill | s0_m_state = m_replay) & s0_m_excl ->
                    exclusive1(s0_m_base)
                invariant [excl_inf_1_p1]
                    (s1_m_state = m_fill | s1_m_state = m_replay) & s1_m_excl ->
                    exclusive1(s1_m_base)

                # ---- the concrete channel-3 register mirrors cachemem. ----
                invariant [ae_sync_p1] ack1_g_en = ae_r

            }

        } with this, dir_body, addr_thy, basis, data_impl
        isolate mc_body = {

            # ---- directory command channel: the command comes from the
            # directory, the ready is driven here ----
            wire cmd_en    : bool
            wire cmd_wen   : bool
            wire cmd_addr  : addr
            wire cmd_data  : four_words
            wire cmd_ready : bool

            # ---- directory response channel: all driven here ----
            wire rsp_valid : bool
            wire rsp_addr  : addr
            wire rsp_data  : four_words

            wire mem_rd_en   : bool
            wire mem_rd_addr : addr
            wire mem_wr_en   : bool
            wire mem_wr_addr : addr
            wire mem_wr_data : four_words
            wire mem_rdata   : four_words

            # ==================================================================
            # The controller.  UNCHANGED from the cache-driven version: same
            # beat-granular port, same two-command buffer, same latency
            # counter, same `mc_owns_img`.  Only the driver of the command
            # wires is different, and `mc_owns_img` never named the driver --
            # it needs `wb_img`, and `wb_img` now arrives from `dir_body`.
            #
            # `mmem` is real hardware -- the DRAM behind the port -- but its
            # client is proved against it, so it lives in the specification
            # where that client can see it.
            #
            # The write is unbuffered and is applied from the command wires
            # alone: a command lands in memory on the edge the port carries
            # it, one edge after it was issued.  The buffer below delays only
            # the *response*, and the write is applied at the top of the
            # edge, so a read never overtakes a write to the same beat.
            # ==================================================================
            specification {
                # The memory that the memory controller presents
                function mmem(H : line, O : woffset) : word

                after init {
                    mmem(H, O) := init_img(H, O);
                }

                # ---- ONE conditional whole-array write: four sequential
                # writes each spawn a quantified frame axiom and they
                # chain, which measured as the dominant instantiation cost
                # in `mem_img`'s consecution.  `wsel4` bridges to the
                # `unp0..3` forms by `p_wsel0..3` under `p_cover`. ----
                after posedge {
                    if cmd_en & cmd_wen {
                        mmem(H, O) := (wsel4(cmd_data, boff2(O))
                                       if (H = high(cmd_addr)
                                           & beat_of(O) = beat_of(low(cmd_addr)))
                                       else mmem(H, O));
                        # (the matching `dirtied0`/`dirtied1` CLEAR lives in
                        # `cachemem`'s monitor, ahead of the store arm, so
                        # a same-edge store-set wins)
                    };
                }

                # ==========================================================
                # Wherever neither cache has dirtied the word, the DRAM is
                # the reference image.  Proven HERE, against the
                # controller's own small
                # transition.  What it needs of its client is `wb_img` --
                # anything put on the command wire is accurate to `img` --
                # and that arrives by IMPORT, through `with this`: `cachemem`
                # presents its children's `specification` blocks, so
                # `dir_body.wb_img` is a pre-state assumption here without
                # `dir_body` being named below.  It is load-bearing: with
                # `wb_img` moved into a `private` block this goal stops
                # closing entirely.
                # ==========================================================

                invariant [mc_owns_img] ~dirtied0(H, O) & ~dirtied1(H, O) ->
                    mmem(H, O) = img(H, O)

                before posedge {
                    assume [dram_read] mem_rd_en ->
                          unp0(mem_rdata) = mmem(high(mem_rd_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(mem_rd_addr)), 0))
                        & unp1(mem_rdata) = mmem(high(mem_rd_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(mem_rd_addr)), 1))
                        & unp2(mem_rdata) = mmem(high(mem_rd_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(mem_rd_addr)), 2))
                        & unp3(mem_rdata) = mmem(high(mem_rd_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(mem_rd_addr)), 3));
                }

                # ---- when the controller raises a response it carries the
                # *current* content of its memory at the address it echoes,
                # not a stale snapshot: the response registers are cleared at
                # the top of every edge and refilled after that edge's write
                # has landed. ----
                invariant [mc_resp] rsp_valid ->
                      unp0(rsp_data) = mmem(high(rsp_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(rsp_addr)), 0))
                    & unp1(rsp_data) = mmem(high(rsp_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(rsp_addr)), 1))
                    & unp2(rsp_data) = mmem(high(rsp_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(rsp_addr)), 2))
                    & unp3(rsp_data) = mmem(high(rsp_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(rsp_addr)), 3))

                # ---- the port never accepts a command on the edge one is
                # already travelling: `cmd_ready` implies the wire is idle.
                # The directory needs this to know that a writeback beat it
                # issued has already been applied by the time the port lets
                # it issue the next one -- which is what makes the two beats
                # of a writeback sequential rather than overlapping. ----
                invariant [rdy_idle] cmd_ready -> ~cmd_en

            }

            implementation {

                # ---- the command buffer ----
                var cb0_valid : bool
                var cb0_wen   : bool
                var cb0_addr  : addr
                var cb1_valid : bool
                var cb1_wen   : bool
                var cb1_addr  : addr
                var svc_count : latency
                wire mem_latency : latency
                definition mem_latency = 8

                # ---- the registers driving the response port ----
                var rsp_valid_r : bool
                var rsp_addr_r  : addr
                var rsp_data_r  : four_words

                after init {
                    cb0_valid := false; cb0_wen := false; cb0_addr := 0;
                    cb1_valid := false; cb1_wen := false; cb1_addr := 0;
                    svc_count := 0;
                    rsp_valid_r := false; rsp_addr_r := 0; rsp_data_r := 0;
                }

                definition cmd_ready = ~(cb0_valid & cb1_valid) & ~cmd_en

                definition rsp_valid = rsp_valid_r
                definition rsp_addr  = rsp_addr_r
                definition rsp_data  = rsp_data_r

                definition mem_rd_en   = cb0_valid & ~cb0_wen & ~rsp_valid & svc_count = 0
                definition mem_rd_addr = cb0_addr
                definition mem_wr_en   = cmd_en & cmd_wen
                definition mem_wr_addr = cmd_addr
                definition mem_wr_data = cmd_data

                after posedge {
                    rsp_valid_r := false;

                    if cmd_en {
                        var a : addr := cmd_addr;
                        if ~cb0_valid {
                            cb0_valid := true; cb0_wen := cmd_wen; cb0_addr := a;
                            svc_count := mem_latency;
                        } else {
                            cb1_valid := true; cb1_wen := cmd_wen; cb1_addr := a;
                        };
                    };

                    if cb0_valid & (cb0_wen | ~rsp_valid) {
                        if svc_count = 0 {
                            if ~cb0_wen {
                                var h : line := hw_high(cb0_addr);
                                var b : bit  := hw_beat_of(hw_low(cb0_addr));
                                rsp_valid_r := true;
                                rsp_addr_r  := cb0_addr;
                                rsp_data_r  := (cmd_data
                                    if (cmd_en & cmd_wen
                                        & hw_high(cmd_addr) = h
                                        & hw_beat_of(hw_low(cmd_addr)) = b)
                                    else mem_rdata);
                            };
                            if cb1_valid {
                                cb0_valid := true; cb0_wen := cb1_wen; cb0_addr := cb1_addr;
                                cb1_valid := false;
                                svc_count := mem_latency;
                            } else {
                                cb0_valid := false;
                            };
                        } else {
                            svc_count := svc_count - 1;
                        }
                    };
                }

            }

        } with this, addr_thy, basis, data_impl


        # ==================================================================
        # The directory: German's home node, now with two clients.
        #
        # It owns no data (decision 4) and dirty data always round-trips DRAM
        # (decision 3), so the whole of it is one current-request register
        # set, a two-beat read ladder, and a two-beat writeback sequencer
        # sharing one command port -- writebacks first.
        #
        # Blocking, single slot everywhere (decision 2): one request start to
        # finish, one grant outstanding, one writeback at a time; channel 3
        # is per CPU and the sequencer serves CPU 0 first.
        #
        # SHARED AND EXCLUSIVE.  Every request is picked; `d_inv` is what
        # makes room for it.  An Exclusive request displaces every holder, a
        # Shared one only the exclusive holder, and both wait for the
        # REQUESTER's own bit as well -- which can only ever be a stale one,
        # since a cache never requests a line it holds.  That last clause is
        # what makes a grant land on a line the directory has no record of
        # the requester at, and it is what `[gr_notack*]` and `[gr_clean*]`
        # rest on.
        #
        # An invalidate is sent only to a cache with no ack of its own in
        # flight: a bit backed by an ack already in the channel needs no
        # invalidate, it clears itself when that ack retires.  With
        # `[d_back*]` -- no stale bits, modulo an in-flight ack -- that makes
        # "the directory only ever invalidates a holder" a theorem rather
        # than a convention.
        # ==================================================================
        isolate dir_body = {

            # ---- the ports this directory drives, per CPU ----
            wire req_taken0 : bool
            wire gr_valid0  : bool
            wire gr_excl0   : bool
            wire gr_line0   : line
            wire gr_data0   : cache_line
            wire inv_valid0 : bool
            wire inv_line0  : line
            wire ack_taken0 : bool
            wire req_taken1 : bool
            wire gr_valid1  : bool
            wire gr_excl1   : bool
            wire gr_line1   : line
            wire gr_data1   : cache_line
            wire inv_valid1 : bool
            wire inv_line1  : line
            wire ack_taken1 : bool

            # ---- memory command channel ----
            wire cmd_en    : bool
            wire cmd_wen   : bool
            wire cmd_addr  : addr
            wire cmd_data  : four_words
            wire cmd_ready : bool

            # ---- memory response channel ----
            wire rsp_valid : bool
            wire rsp_addr  : addr
            wire rsp_data  : four_words

            # ==============================================================
            # The contract.
            #
            # `[swmr]` is the coherence property, and it is what each cache's
            # data invariants rely on: with it, a store by one CPU cannot
            # move `img` under a line the other is holding.  The chain is
            # acyclic -- the caches' seam facts are cache-local and assume no
            # protocol, the directory consumes them and returns SWMR, and the
            # caches consume SWMR only for their data half.
            # ==============================================================
            specification {
                function dir(H : line, O : woffset) : word
                definition dir(H, O) = mc_body.mmem(H, O)

                invariant [dir_img] ~dirtied0(H, O) & ~dirtied1(H, O) ->
                    dir(H, O) = img(H, O)

                # Shared holders may coexist.  Only an Exclusive holder
                # excludes every copy in the other cache.
                invariant [swmr0] exclusive0(H) -> ~holds1(H)
                invariant [swmr1] exclusive1(H) -> ~holds0(H)

                # Granted data is accurate
                invariant [grant_img_lo0] c0_gr_valid ->
                    lsel(c0_gr_data, concatbv2(0, J)) = img(c0_gr_line, concatbv2(0, J))
                invariant [grant_img_hi0] c0_gr_valid ->
                    lsel(c0_gr_data, concatbv2(1, J)) = img(c0_gr_line, concatbv2(1, J))
                invariant [grant_img_lo1] c1_gr_valid ->
                    lsel(c1_gr_data, concatbv2(0, J)) = img(c1_gr_line, concatbv2(0, J))
                invariant [grant_img_hi1] c1_gr_valid ->
                    lsel(c1_gr_data, concatbv2(1, J)) = img(c1_gr_line, concatbv2(1, J))

                invariant [wb_img] cmd_en & cmd_wen ->
                    wsel4(cmd_data, J) =
                        img(high(cmd_addr), concatbv2(beat_of(low(cmd_addr)), J))

                invariant [wb_done0] c0_ack_taken ->
                    ~dirtied0(ack0_g_line, O) & ~dirtied1(ack0_g_line, O)
                invariant [wb_done1] c1_ack_taken ->
                    ~dirtied0(ack1_g_line, O) & ~dirtied1(ack1_g_line, O)

                invariant [gr_notack0] c0_gr_valid ->
                    ~(ack0_g_en & ack0_g_line = c0_gr_line)
                invariant [gr_notack1] c1_gr_valid ->
                    ~(ack1_g_en & ack1_g_line = c1_gr_line)
                invariant [gr_clean0] c0_gr_valid ->
                    ~dirtied0(c0_gr_line, O) & ~dirtied1(c0_gr_line, O)
                invariant [gr_clean1] c1_gr_valid ->
                    ~dirtied0(c1_gr_line, O) & ~dirtied1(c1_gr_line, O)

                invariant [gr_excl0i] c0_gr_valid & c0_gr_excl ->
                    ~holds1(c0_gr_line)
                invariant [gr_excl1i] c1_gr_valid & c1_gr_excl ->
                    ~holds0(c1_gr_line)
                invariant [gr_shared0i] c0_gr_valid & ~c0_gr_excl ->
                    ~exclusive1(c0_gr_line)
                invariant [gr_shared1i] c1_gr_valid & ~c1_gr_excl ->
                    ~exclusive0(c1_gr_line)

                invariant [ack_excl0] ack0_g_en & ack0_g_dty ->
                    ~holds1(ack0_g_line)
                invariant [ack_excl1] ack1_g_en & ack1_g_dty ->
                    ~holds0(ack1_g_line)

                # ---- German's at-most-one-exclusive corollary, over the
                # permission ghosts: `~(cacheExclusive(C) & cacheExclusive(D))`
                # for the two clients.  Falls out of `[d_ex*]` + `[dex_one]`. ----
                invariant [g_one_excl] ~(exclusive0(H) & exclusive1(H))

                # ---- `[commit_safe]`: exclusivity empties every other
                # holder.  Logically nothing beyond SWMR -- it is the
                # single-writer clause of it -- and it is kept as its own
                # named invariant purely as the isolate-boundary cut, so every
                # `*_img` preservation case cites ONE lemma instead of
                # re-deriving the backbone.  A store commit is an instance:
                # `[ca_st_excl_*]` turns `c0_st_en` into `exclusive0(c0_st_h)`.
                #
                # Keyed on the permission ghost and NOT on the commit wire
                # itself: `c0_st_en` is driven by a cache definition this
                # isolate cannot see, so in the post-state it is an
                # unconstrained boolean and an invariant guarded by it is not
                # provable here at all (measured: the query does not fail, it
                # runs for minutes).  The commit-edge reading is recovered at
                # the cache, where the wire has a definition. ----
                invariant [commit_safe0] exclusive0(H) ->
                      ~holds1(H)
                    & ~(ack1_g_en & ack1_g_dty & ack1_g_line = H)
                    & ~(c1_gr_valid & c1_gr_line = H)
                invariant [commit_safe1] exclusive1(H) ->
                      ~holds0(H)
                    & ~(ack0_g_en & ack0_g_dty & ack0_g_line = H)
                    & ~(c0_gr_valid & c0_gr_line = H)
            }

            implementation {
                wire d_idle   : dstate
                wire d_inv    : dstate
                wire d_rd_lo  : dstate
                wire d_rdw_lo : dstate
                wire d_rd_hi  : dstate
                wire d_rdw_hi : dstate
                wire d_grant  : dstate
                wire d_gwt    : dstate
                definition d_idle   = 0
                definition d_inv    = 1
                definition d_rd_lo  = 2
                definition d_rdw_lo = 3
                definition d_rd_hi  = 4
                definition d_rdw_hi = 5
                definition d_grant  = 6
                definition d_gwt    = 7

                wire wb_idle : wbstate
                wire wb_lo   : wbstate
                wire wb_hi   : wbstate
                wire wb_fin  : wbstate
                wire wb_ret  : wbstate
                definition wb_idle = 0
                definition wb_lo   = 1
                definition wb_hi   = 2
                definition wb_fin  = 3
                definition wb_ret  = 4

                # ---- the current request, and who still has to give the
                # line up before it can be served ----
                var dstate_r  : dstate
                var cr_cpu    : bit
                var cr_line   : line
                var cr_excl   : bool
                var inv_need0 : bool
                var inv_need1 : bool
                var dfb_lo    : four_words
                var dfb_hi    : four_words

                # ---- the one grant slot, routed to `cr_cpu` ----
                var gr_v : bool
                var gr_x : bool
                var gr_l : line
                var gr_d : cache_line

                # ---- one held invalidate slot per cache ----
                var inv0_v : bool
                var inv0_l : line
                var inv1_v : bool
                var inv1_l : line

                # ---- the writeback sequencer.  `wb_lo_done`/`wb_hi_done`
                # are GHOST: the beat has LANDED in memory, not merely been
                # issued. ----
                var wb_st      : wbstate
                var wb_cpu     : bit
                var wb_line    : line
                var wb_lo_done : bool
                var wb_hi_done : bool

                # ==========================================================
                # The sharer bits, and ONE exclusive bit beside them: the
                # owner is not stored, it is `dex(H) & dshC(H)` -- exactly
                # one sharer bit can be set under `dex` (`[dex_one]`), so
                # naming the owner with a `bit` register and comparing it
                # against `wb_cpu` bought nothing and cost the solver a
                # great deal (with an owner index, `[wb_done0]` stopped
                # coming back).  It is also what makes the retire arm's
                # clear unconditional: a cache with a sharer bit and an ack
                # in flight IS the exclusive holder if there is one.
                #
                # The bits are EXACT in the direction the protocol needs: a
                # line with no bit is in no cache, and a line with no
                # exclusive bit is writable by nobody.
                # ==========================================================
                var dsh0(H : line) : bool
                var dsh1(H : line) : bool
                var dex(H : line)  : bool

                # ---- the command port's registers ----
                var ev_cmd      : bool
                var ev_cmd_wen  : bool
                var ev_cmd_addr : addr
                var ev_cmd_data : four_words

                after init {
                    dsh0(H) := false; dsh1(H) := false;
                    dex(H) := false;
                    dstate_r := 0; cr_cpu := 0; cr_line := 0; cr_excl := false;
                    inv_need0 := false; inv_need1 := false;
                    dfb_lo := 0; dfb_hi := 0;
                    gr_v := false; gr_x := false; gr_l := 0; gr_d := 0;
                    inv0_v := false; inv0_l := 0;
                    inv1_v := false; inv1_l := 0;
                    wb_st := 0; wb_cpu := 0; wb_line := 0;
                    wb_lo_done := false; wb_hi_done := false;
                    ev_cmd := false; ev_cmd_wen := false;
                    ev_cmd_addr := 0; ev_cmd_data := 0;
                }

                definition gr_valid0 = gr_v & cr_cpu = 0
                definition gr_excl0  = gr_x
                definition gr_line0  = gr_l
                definition gr_data0  = gr_d
                definition gr_valid1 = gr_v & cr_cpu = 1
                definition gr_excl1  = gr_x
                definition gr_line1  = gr_l
                definition gr_data1  = gr_d
                definition inv_valid0 = inv0_v
                definition inv_line0  = inv0_l
                definition inv_valid1 = inv1_v
                definition inv_line1  = inv1_l

                # ---- EVERY request is picked; `d_inv` is what makes room
                # for it.  CPU 0 first; fairness is a non-goal. ----
                wire pick_ok : bool
                definition pick_ok = (dstate_r = d_idle) & (wb_st = wb_idle) & ~gr_v
                definition req_taken0 = pick_ok & c0_req_en
                definition req_taken1 = pick_ok & c1_req_en & ~c0_req_en

                definition ack_taken0 = (wb_st = wb_ret) & wb_cpu = 0
                definition ack_taken1 = (wb_st = wb_ret) & wb_cpu = 1

                definition cmd_en   = ev_cmd
                definition cmd_wen  = ev_cmd_wen
                definition cmd_addr = ev_cmd_addr
                definition cmd_data = ev_cmd_data

                after posedge {
                    # ---- GHOST.  The command presented on the last edge is
                    # applied to memory on THIS one. ----
                    if ev_cmd & ev_cmd_wen & hw_high(ev_cmd_addr) = wb_line {
                        if hw_beat_of(hw_low(ev_cmd_addr)) = 0 {
                            wb_lo_done := true;
                        } else {
                            wb_hi_done := true;
                        };
                    };

                    ev_cmd := false;

                    # ---- an invalidate retires when its cache posts the
                    # reply; the reply itself drains through channel 3. ----
                    if inv0_v & c0_inv_taken { inv0_v := false; };
                    if inv1_v & c1_inv_taken { inv1_v := false; };

                    # ---- channel 2 retires, and the bits go up: the sharer
                    # bit always, the exclusive bit for an Exclusive grant. ----
                    if gr_v & ((cr_cpu = 0 & c0_gr_taken)
                               | (cr_cpu = 1 & c1_gr_taken)) {
                        if cr_cpu = 0 {
                            dsh0(H) := (true if H = gr_l else dsh0(H));
                        } else {
                            dsh1(H) := (true if H = gr_l else dsh1(H));
                        };
                        if gr_x {
                            dex(H) := (true if H = gr_l else dex(H));
                        };
                        gr_v := false;
                        if dstate_r = d_gwt {
                            dstate_r := d_idle;
                        };
                    };

                    # ---- the read ladder. ----
                    if rsp_valid & hw_high(rsp_addr) = cr_line {
                        if dstate_r = d_rdw_lo & hw_beat_of(hw_low(rsp_addr)) = 0 {
                            dfb_lo   := rsp_data;
                            dstate_r := d_rd_hi;
                        } else { if dstate_r = d_rdw_hi & hw_beat_of(hw_low(rsp_addr)) = 1 {
                            dfb_hi   := rsp_data;
                            dstate_r := d_grant;
                        } };
                    };

                    # ---- the one command port.  Writeback beats first, and
                    # AHEAD of the channel-3 pickup below so a beat is never
                    # issued on the edge its ack was posted. ----
                    if cmd_ready {
                        if wb_st = wb_lo {
                            ev_cmd      := true;
                            ev_cmd_wen  := true;
                            ev_cmd_addr := hw_concatbv3(wb_line, hw_concatbv2(0, 0));
                            ev_cmd_data := hw_llo(c0_ack_dat if wb_cpu = 0
                                                  else c1_ack_dat);
                            wb_st       := wb_hi;
                        } else { if wb_st = wb_hi {
                            ev_cmd      := true;
                            ev_cmd_wen  := true;
                            ev_cmd_addr := hw_concatbv3(wb_line, hw_concatbv2(1, 0));
                            ev_cmd_data := hw_lhi(c0_ack_dat if wb_cpu = 0
                                                  else c1_ack_dat);
                            wb_st       := wb_fin;
                        } else { if dstate_r = d_rd_lo {
                            ev_cmd      := true;
                            ev_cmd_wen  := false;
                            ev_cmd_addr := hw_concatbv3(cr_line, hw_concatbv2(0, 0));
                            ev_cmd_data := 0;
                            dstate_r    := d_rdw_lo;
                        } else { if dstate_r = d_rd_hi {
                            ev_cmd      := true;
                            ev_cmd_wen  := false;
                            ev_cmd_addr := hw_concatbv3(cr_line, hw_concatbv2(1, 0));
                            ev_cmd_data := 0;
                            dstate_r    := d_rdw_hi;
                        } } } };
                    };

                    # ---- channel 3 is always on -- an ack answers an
                    # invalidate, a self-downgrade or a fill-eviction, and
                    # the arm cannot tell them apart.  CPU 0 first. ----
                    if wb_st = wb_idle {
                        if c0_ack_en {
                            wb_cpu     := 0;
                            wb_line    := c0_ack_line;
                            wb_lo_done := false;
                            wb_hi_done := false;
                            if c0_ack_dty { wb_st := wb_lo; }
                            else { wb_st := wb_ret; };
                        } else { if c1_ack_en {
                            wb_cpu     := 1;
                            wb_line    := c1_ack_line;
                            wb_lo_done := false;
                            wb_hi_done := false;
                            if c1_ack_dty { wb_st := wb_lo; }
                            else { wb_st := wb_ret; };
                        } };
                    } else { if wb_st = wb_fin & wb_lo_done & wb_hi_done {
                        wb_st := wb_ret;
                    } else { if wb_st = wb_ret {
                        dex(H) := (false if H = wb_line else dex(H));
                        if wb_cpu = 0 {
                            dsh0(H) := (false if H = wb_line else dsh0(H));
                            if dstate_r = d_inv & cr_line = wb_line {
                                inv_need0 := false;
                            };
                            if inv0_v & inv0_l = wb_line { inv0_v := false; };
                        } else {
                            dsh1(H) := (false if H = wb_line else dsh1(H));
                            if dstate_r = d_inv & cr_line = wb_line {
                                inv_need1 := false;
                            };
                            if inv1_v & inv1_l = wb_line { inv1_v := false; };
                        };
                        wb_st      := wb_idle;
                        wb_lo_done := false;
                        wb_hi_done := false;
                    } } };

                    # ---- make room for the request.  An invalidate is sent
                    # only to a cache with no ack of its own in flight: a bit
                    # backed by an ack already in the channel needs no
                    # invalidate, it clears itself when that ack retires.
                    #
                    # The wait is on the BITS, never on the invalidate slots,
                    # and it always includes the requester's own bit -- which
                    # can only be a stale one, since a cache never requests a
                    # line it holds.  That is what makes the grant land on a
                    # line with no record of the requester at all. ----
                    if dstate_r = d_inv {
                        if inv_need0 & ~inv0_v & ~c0_ack_en {
                            inv0_v := true; inv0_l := cr_line;
                            inv_need0 := false;
                        };
                        if inv_need1 & ~inv1_v & ~c1_ack_en {
                            inv1_v := true; inv1_l := cr_line;
                            inv_need1 := false;
                        };
                        if cr_excl {
                            if ~dsh0(cr_line) & ~dsh1(cr_line) {
                                inv_need0 := false; inv_need1 := false;
                                dstate_r := d_rd_lo;
                            };
                        } else {
                            if cr_cpu = 0 {
                                if ~dsh0(cr_line) & ~dex(cr_line) {
                                    inv_need0 := false; inv_need1 := false;
                                    dstate_r := d_rd_lo;
                                };
                            } else {
                                if ~dsh1(cr_line) & ~dex(cr_line) {
                                    inv_need0 := false; inv_need1 := false;
                                    dstate_r := d_rd_lo;
                                };
                            };
                        };
                    };

                    # ---- the grant ----
                    if dstate_r = d_grant {
                        gr_v     := true;
                        gr_x     := cr_excl;
                        gr_l     := cr_line;
                        gr_d     := hw_lpack(dfb_hi, dfb_lo);
                        dstate_r := d_gwt;
                    };

                    # ---- pick a new request.  Last, so the slot a grant
                    # freed this edge is not reused on the same edge.  An
                    # Exclusive request must displace every holder; a Shared
                    # one only the exclusive holder. ----
                    if dstate_r = d_idle & wb_st = wb_idle & ~gr_v {
                        if c0_req_en {
                            cr_cpu   := 0;
                            cr_line  := c0_req_line;
                            cr_excl  := c0_req_excl;
                            inv_need0 := dsh0(c0_req_line)
                                         & (c0_req_excl | dex(c0_req_line));
                            inv_need1 := dsh1(c0_req_line)
                                         & (c0_req_excl | dex(c0_req_line));
                            dstate_r := d_inv;
                        } else { if c1_req_en {
                            cr_cpu   := 1;
                            cr_line  := c1_req_line;
                            cr_excl  := c1_req_excl;
                            inv_need0 := dsh0(c1_req_line)
                                         & (c1_req_excl | dex(c1_req_line));
                            inv_need1 := dsh1(c1_req_line)
                                         & (c1_req_excl | dex(c1_req_line));
                            dstate_r := d_inv;
                        } };
                    };
                }

                # ==========================================================
                # 6.1  Structure of the two sequencers.  Unchanged from the
                # exclusive-only directory: channel 3 never knew what kind of
                # surrender it was carrying.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [d_gr_state] gr_v -> dstate_r = d_gwt
                invariant [d_gwt_gr] dstate_r = d_gwt -> gr_v

                invariant [wb_ack] wb_st ~= wb_idle & wb_st ~= wb_ret ->
                      (wb_cpu = 0 & ack0_g_en & ack0_g_dty & ack0_g_line = wb_line)
                    | (wb_cpu = 1 & ack1_g_en & ack1_g_dty & ack1_g_line = wb_line)
                invariant [wb_ret_line] wb_st = wb_ret ->
                      (wb_cpu = 0 & ack0_g_en & ack0_g_line = wb_line)
                    | (wb_cpu = 1 & ack1_g_en & ack1_g_line = wb_line)
                invariant [wb_ret_clean] wb_st = wb_ret ->
                    ~dirtied0(wb_line, O) & ~dirtied1(wb_line, O)

                invariant [wb_cmd] ev_cmd & ev_cmd_wen ->
                    high(ev_cmd_addr) = wb_line
                    & ((wb_st = wb_hi & beat_of(low(ev_cmd_addr)) = 0)
                       | (wb_st = wb_fin & beat_of(low(ev_cmd_addr)) = 1))
                invariant [wb_hi_late] wb_hi_done
                    -> wb_st = wb_fin | wb_st = wb_ret
                invariant [rd_cmd] ev_cmd & ~ev_cmd_wen ->
                    (dstate_r = d_rdw_lo | dstate_r = d_rdw_hi)
                    & high(ev_cmd_addr) = cr_line

                invariant [wb_lo_clean] wb_lo_done & beat_of(O) = 0
                    -> ~dirtied0(wb_line, O) & ~dirtied1(wb_line, O)
                invariant [wb_hi_clean] wb_hi_done & beat_of(O) = 1
                    -> ~dirtied0(wb_line, O) & ~dirtied1(wb_line, O)
                invariant [wb_ghost_idle] wb_st = wb_idle
                    -> ~wb_lo_done & ~wb_hi_done

                # ==========================================================
                # 6.2  The bits are exact, and the exclusive bit is what
                # SWMR now rests on.
                #
                # `[dex_sh*]` is the whole coherence argument in one line: an
                # exclusive bit excludes the other cache's sharer bit, and
                # `[d_exact*]` turns that into "the other cache does not hold
                # the line".  Shared bits are free to coexist.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [d_exact0] holds0(H) -> dsh0(H)
                invariant [d_exact1] holds1(H) -> dsh1(H)
                invariant [dsh_ack0] ack0_g_en -> dsh0(ack0_g_line)
                invariant [dsh_ack1] ack1_g_en -> dsh1(ack1_g_line)

                # ---- and the converse: no STALE bits.  A set bit is backed
                # by a copy the cache still holds, or by an ack already in the
                # channel.  With the send guard (`~ackN_g_en`) this is what
                # makes "the directory only invalidates a holder" a theorem:
                # `[inv_live*]` says a live invalidate names a line whose bit
                # is set, and this says such a line is really there. ----
                invariant [d_back0] dsh0(H) ->
                    holds0(H) | (ack0_g_en & ack0_g_line = H)
                invariant [d_back1] dsh1(H) ->
                    holds1(H) | (ack1_g_en & ack1_g_line = H)
                # ==========================================================
                # 6.5  German's backbone, transcribed per line.
                #
                # These are the classic invariants of `german.ivy` /
                # `german_evict.ivy` restated over this file's holder
                # relations, and they are the audit trail for the
                # correspondence rather than load-bearing lemmas -- each is a
                # consequence of the exactness block above.  They are stated
                # HERE and not in the specification only because `dsh*`/`dex`/
                # `dstate_r`/`inv*_v` are private to this section.
                #
                #   german.ivy                            this file
                #   ------------------------------------  ---------------------
                #   cacheShared(C) -> homeSharerList(C)   [g_cacheS_dir*]
                #     & ~homeExclusiveGranted
                #   cacheExclusive(C) -> homeSharerList   [d_ex0], [d_ex1]
                #     & homeExclusiveGranted & C = owner
                #   homeExclusiveGranted                  [dex_owned], [dex_one]
                #     & homeSharerList(C) -> C = owner
                #   invalidateAck(C) -> ~cacheS/E(C)      [ca_ack_idle_p*]
                #     & homeSharerList(C)                 [dsh_ack0], [dsh_ack1]
                #   invalidate(C) -> homeCurrentReq       [g_inv_of_cr*]
                #     & homeSharerList(C)                 [inv_live0], [inv_live1]
                #   ~(invalidate(C) & grant*(C))          [g_no_gr_inv*]
                #   homeSharerList(C) -> C holds it or a  [d_back0], [d_back1]
                #     message for it is in flight
                #   at most one exclusive                 [g_one_excl] (exported)
                #   grant answers the picked request      [grant_kind]
                # ==========================================================

                # ---- an exclusive bit has an owner: some sharer bit backs
                # it, and `[dex_one]` says only one can. ----
                invariant [dex_owned] dex(H) -> dsh0(H) | dsh1(H)

                # ---- a SHARED holder implies nobody holds the line
                # exclusively.  The other direction of `[d_ex*]`, and the
                # concrete form of `cacheShared -> ~homeExclusiveGranted`. ----
                invariant [g_cacheS_dir0] holds0(H) & ~exclusive0(H) -> ~dex(H)
                invariant [g_cacheS_dir1] holds1(H) & ~exclusive1(H) -> ~dex(H)

                # ---- an invalidate belongs to the request in progress: it is
                # posted in `d_inv` for `cr_line` and retired before the
                # transaction can leave `d_inv`, so the stale-invalidate case
                # the eviction extension had to absorb abstractly cannot arise
                # here.  With it, channel 2's grant and the invalidate slot are
                # mutually exclusive per CPU. ----
                invariant [g_inv_of_cr0] inv0_v -> dstate_r = d_inv & inv0_l = cr_line
                invariant [g_inv_of_cr1] inv1_v -> dstate_r = d_inv & inv1_l = cr_line
                invariant [g_no_gr_inv0] gr_v & cr_cpu = 0 -> ~inv0_v
                invariant [g_no_gr_inv1] gr_v & cr_cpu = 1 -> ~inv1_v

                # ---- a pending invalidate still names a set bit: that is
                # what carries "the bit was set when the request was picked"
                # forward to the edge the invalidate is actually sent. ----
                # The `(cr_excl | dex ...)` clause is the CONFLICT the
                # invalidate was sent for, carried alongside it: without it a
                # Shared request could reach `d_rd_lo` -- whose completion test
                # only names the exclusive bit -- while the other cache's
                # invalidate slot was still live.
                invariant [inv_need0_ok] inv_need0 ->
                    dstate_r = d_inv & dsh0(cr_line)
                    & (cr_excl | dex(cr_line))
                invariant [inv_need1_ok] inv_need1 ->
                    dstate_r = d_inv & dsh1(cr_line)
                    & (cr_excl | dex(cr_line))
                invariant [inv_live0] inv0_v ->
                    dsh0(inv0_l) & (cr_excl | dex(inv0_l))
                invariant [inv_live1] inv1_v ->
                    dsh1(inv1_l) & (cr_excl | dex(inv1_l))

                invariant [d_ex0] exclusive0(H) -> dex(H) & dsh0(H)
                invariant [d_ex1] exclusive1(H) -> dex(H) & dsh1(H)
                invariant [dex_one] dex(H) -> ~(dsh0(H) & dsh1(H))

                # ---- a dirty ack comes from the cache that had write
                # permission, and its exclusive bit is still set: the home
                # has not consumed the ack yet. ----
                invariant [dirty_ack_ex0] ack0_g_en & ack0_g_dty ->
                    dex(ack0_g_line)
                invariant [dirty_ack_ex1] ack1_g_en & ack1_g_dty ->
                    dex(ack1_g_line)

                # ==========================================================
                # 6.3  The request in progress.
                #
                # `[rd_perm]` is what the whole `d_inv` phase exists to
                # establish, and it holds from the edge the read ladder
                # starts to the edge the grant is consumed: the bits it names
                # can only rise on a grant consumption, and there is one
                # grant slot.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [rd_perm] dstate_r >= d_rd_lo ->
                      (cr_excl & ~dsh0(cr_line) & ~dsh1(cr_line))
                    | (~cr_excl & ~dex(cr_line)
                       & ((cr_cpu = 0 & ~dsh0(cr_line))
                          | (cr_cpu = 1 & ~dsh1(cr_line))))

                # ---- `[grant_kind]`: a grant in ch2(C) answers C's picked
                # request -- same line, same Shared/Exclusive kind.  The
                # routing half is syntactic (`gr_valid0 = gr_v & cr_cpu = 0`),
                # and this is what lets a fill set `tag_exc` off the grant and
                # `[rq_store_excl_*]` trust an Exclusive one. ----
                invariant [grant_kind] gr_v -> gr_l = cr_line & gr_x = cr_excl

                # ---- `[rd_clean]`: no word of a line under request is dirtied.
                # A store needs the exclusive bit (`[ca_st_excl_*]` through
                # `[d_ex*]`), and `[rd_perm]` has cleared it. ----
                invariant [rd_clean] dstate_r >= d_rd_lo ->
                    ~dirtied0(cr_line, O) & ~dirtied1(cr_line, O)

                # ---- the mid-fetch clause of `[commit_safe]`: the line the
                # directory is fetching is nobody's to write. ----
                invariant [commit_notfetch0] dstate_r >= d_rd_lo & exclusive0(H) ->
                    cr_line ~= H
                invariant [commit_notfetch1] dstate_r >= d_rd_lo & exclusive1(H) ->
                    cr_line ~= H

                # ==========================================================
                # 6.4  The fetch buffers, and the grant they become.
                # ==========================================================
                invariant [dfb_img_lo] dstate_r >= d_rd_hi ->
                    wsel4(dfb_lo, J) = img(cr_line, concatbv2(0, J))
                invariant [dfb_img_hi] dstate_r >= d_grant ->
                    wsel4(dfb_hi, J) = img(cr_line, concatbv2(1, J))
            }

        } with this, mc_body, cache0_body, cache1_body, addr_thy, basis, data_impl

        # ---- each net aliased to the port that drives it, and to nothing
        # else: the far side reads the net itself. ----
        definition c0_req_en    = cache0_body.req_en
        definition c0_req_line  = cache0_body.req_line
        definition c0_req_excl  = cache0_body.req_excl
        definition c0_req_taken = dir_body.req_taken0
        definition c0_gr_valid  = dir_body.gr_valid0
        definition c0_gr_excl   = dir_body.gr_excl0
        definition c0_gr_line   = dir_body.gr_line0
        definition c0_gr_data   = dir_body.gr_data0
        definition c0_gr_taken  = cache0_body.gr_taken
        definition c0_inv_valid = dir_body.inv_valid0
        definition c0_inv_line  = dir_body.inv_line0
        definition c0_inv_taken = cache0_body.inv_taken
        definition c0_ack_en   = cache0_body.ack_en
        definition c0_ack_line = cache0_body.ack_line
        definition c0_ack_dty  = cache0_body.ack_dty
        definition c0_ack_dat  = cache0_body.ack_dat
        definition c0_ack_taken = dir_body.ack_taken0
        definition c0_st_en     = cache0_body.st_en
        definition c0_st_h      = cache0_body.st_h
        definition c0_st_o      = cache0_body.st_o
        definition c0_st_d      = cache0_body.st_d
        definition c0_give_en   = cache0_body.give_en
        definition c0_give_line = cache0_body.give_line
        definition c0_give_dty  = cache0_body.give_dty
        definition c0_give_dat  = cache0_body.give_dat

        definition c1_req_en    = cache1_body.req_en
        definition c1_req_line  = cache1_body.req_line
        definition c1_req_excl  = cache1_body.req_excl
        definition c1_req_taken = dir_body.req_taken1
        definition c1_gr_valid  = dir_body.gr_valid1
        definition c1_gr_excl   = dir_body.gr_excl1
        definition c1_gr_line   = dir_body.gr_line1
        definition c1_gr_data   = dir_body.gr_data1
        definition c1_gr_taken  = cache1_body.gr_taken
        definition c1_inv_valid = dir_body.inv_valid1
        definition c1_inv_line  = dir_body.inv_line1
        definition c1_inv_taken = cache1_body.inv_taken
        definition c1_ack_en   = cache1_body.ack_en
        definition c1_ack_line = cache1_body.ack_line
        definition c1_ack_dty  = cache1_body.ack_dty
        definition c1_ack_dat  = cache1_body.ack_dat
        definition c1_ack_taken = dir_body.ack_taken1
        definition c1_st_en     = cache1_body.st_en
        definition c1_st_h      = cache1_body.st_h
        definition c1_st_o      = cache1_body.st_o
        definition c1_st_d      = cache1_body.st_d
        definition c1_give_en   = cache1_body.give_en
        definition c1_give_line = cache1_body.give_line
        definition c1_give_dty  = cache1_body.give_dty
        definition c1_give_dat  = cache1_body.give_dat

        # ---- the two LSQ-side channels ----
        definition cache0_body.lsq_to_cache_req_en0    = lsq_to_cache_req_en0
        definition cache0_body.lsq_to_cache_req_h0     = lsq_to_cache_req_h0
        definition cache0_body.lsq_to_cache_req_o0     = lsq_to_cache_req_o0
        definition cache0_body.lsq_to_cache_req_wen0   = lsq_to_cache_req_wen0
        definition cache0_body.lsq_to_cache_req_wdata0 = lsq_to_cache_req_wdata0
        definition cache0_body.lsq_to_cache_req_idx0   = lsq_to_cache_req_idx0
        definition cache_to_lsq_creq_ready0 = cache0_body.cache_to_lsq_creq_ready0
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0 = cache0_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_valid0
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_h0     = cache0_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_h0
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_o0     = cache0_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_o0
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_data0  = cache0_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_data0
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_idx0   = cache0_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_idx0
        definition cache1_body.lsq_to_cache_req_en1    = lsq_to_cache_req_en1
        definition cache1_body.lsq_to_cache_req_h1     = lsq_to_cache_req_h1
        definition cache1_body.lsq_to_cache_req_o1     = lsq_to_cache_req_o1
        definition cache1_body.lsq_to_cache_req_wen1   = lsq_to_cache_req_wen1
        definition cache1_body.lsq_to_cache_req_wdata1 = lsq_to_cache_req_wdata1
        definition cache1_body.lsq_to_cache_req_idx1   = lsq_to_cache_req_idx1
        definition cache_to_lsq_creq_ready1 = cache1_body.cache_to_lsq_creq_ready1
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1 = cache1_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_valid1
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_h1     = cache1_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_h1
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_o1     = cache1_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_o1
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_data1  = cache1_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_data1
        definition cache_to_lsq_resp_idx1   = cache1_body.cache_to_lsq_resp_idx1

        # ---- the directory's command port drives the memory controller's ----
        definition mc_body.cmd_en    = dir_body.cmd_en
        definition mc_body.cmd_wen   = dir_body.cmd_wen
        definition mc_body.cmd_addr  = dir_body.cmd_addr
        definition mc_body.cmd_data  = dir_body.cmd_data

        # ---- the memory controller's response port drives the directory's ----
        definition dir_body.cmd_ready = mc_body.cmd_ready
        definition dir_body.rsp_valid = mc_body.rsp_valid
        definition dir_body.rsp_addr  = mc_body.rsp_addr
        definition dir_body.rsp_data  = mc_body.rsp_data

        # ---- and the memory-side port on out ----
        definition mem_rd_en   = mc_body.mem_rd_en
        definition mem_rd_addr = mc_body.mem_rd_addr
        definition mem_wr_en   = mc_body.mem_wr_en
        definition mem_wr_addr = mc_body.mem_wr_addr
        definition mem_wr_data = mc_body.mem_wr_data
        definition mc_body.mem_rdata = mem_rdata
    } with this, addr_thy, basis, data_impl
